WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:05.610
All right.

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:07.610
So today's going
to be the second

00:00:07.610 --> 00:00:10.460
of the basic systems lectures.

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:15.060
And now, we're going to move on
to multi-machine optimization.

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:18.710
And so the focus today is going
to be all about parallelism

00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:21.290
across machines.

00:00:21.290 --> 00:00:23.660
And so the goal today
is going to move

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:28.340
from optimizing a single
GPU's throughput to being

00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:31.070
able to understand the
complexities and the details

00:00:31.070 --> 00:00:33.660
that are required to
train really large models.

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:37.710
And when models get large, they
no longer fit on a single GPU.

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:39.590
So you've got to
split up your models

00:00:39.590 --> 00:00:42.380
across different
machines, but also, you've

00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:45.740
got to be able to leverage
all of the different servers

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:48.390
that you have in order to
train these models quickly.

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:50.750
So we've got both compute
and memory concerns

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:52.370
that we're going to
have to deal with.

00:00:52.370 --> 00:00:55.530
And communication across
different machines,

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:57.150
it's going to be
quite heterogeneous.

00:00:57.150 --> 00:00:59.770
We have different kinds of
communication across GPUs

00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:01.670
at different levels
of hierarchy.

00:01:01.670 --> 00:01:04.180
And so this is going to lead
to different parallelization

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:05.239
paradigms.

00:01:05.239 --> 00:01:08.290
People use many different
parallelization strategies

00:01:08.290 --> 00:01:09.413
all together at once.

00:01:09.413 --> 00:01:11.080
And we're going to
talk through each one

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:13.060
of the very popular ones.

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:15.730
And then we'll talk about
how you combine them together

00:01:15.730 --> 00:01:18.418
in order to efficiently
train a very large model.

00:01:18.418 --> 00:01:19.960
And then I'm going
to end the lecture

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:23.770
with looking at some examples
of how people are actually

00:01:23.770 --> 00:01:27.730
using these parallelization
strategies to run

00:01:27.730 --> 00:01:32.071
their large scale
distributed training runs.

00:01:32.071 --> 00:01:34.210
And so that's going
to roughly map

00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:35.900
to the different
parts of this lecture.

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:38.630
We're just going to talk about
the basics of networking first,

00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:40.720
and then we're going
to talk about how

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:43.570
do each of these networking
hardware concepts

00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:46.550
map to different
parallelization strategies

00:01:46.550 --> 00:01:48.610
and then finally,
some case studies

00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:52.691
to close off with to show you
how it all comes together.

00:01:52.691 --> 00:01:56.240
So I told you about
GPU scaling last week,

00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:00.310
and it's quite impressive seeing
this super exponential curve

00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:03.200
of flops per GPU
going way, way up.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:08.660
But if we want to rapidly scale
out both our compute and memory,

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:10.389
a single GPU isn't enough.

00:02:10.389 --> 00:02:13.090
We're going to have to
wait for another couple

00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:16.645
years for this curve to continue
going upwards and upwards

00:02:16.645 --> 00:02:17.240
and upwards.

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:20.650
So if we want to train a really
powerful language model here

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:23.440
and now today, well,
we have to rely

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:25.250
on multi-machine parallelism.

00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:28.270
So if we look at the world's
fastest supercomputers, that's

00:02:28.270 --> 00:02:30.290
what's being shown
on the right here,

00:02:30.290 --> 00:02:33.640
the fastest supercomputers
have exaflops and exaflops

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:34.720
of compute.

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:37.505
Those are the green lines
that you see over there.

00:02:37.505 --> 00:02:39.130
That's what you're
really going to have

00:02:39.130 --> 00:02:40.510
to rely on if
you're going to try

00:02:40.510 --> 00:02:45.370
to train the biggest and
baddest language models today.

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:47.530
And so that's the
compute side of why

00:02:47.530 --> 00:02:50.150
you want to think about
multi-machine parallelism.

00:02:50.150 --> 00:02:52.150
But we've also
got a memory angle

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:53.660
for thinking about
the same thing.

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:56.522
So these two are really the core
resources and the core concerns

00:02:56.522 --> 00:02:58.230
that you're going to
have to think about.

00:02:58.230 --> 00:03:00.920
So in terms of memory,
many of the models

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:02.130
are getting quite big.

00:03:02.130 --> 00:03:05.450
And of course, memory on GPUs
is also growing but not quite

00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:06.210
as quickly.

00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:10.020
And a single GPU is not going
to be able to fit these models.

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:11.700
Maybe eventually in
the distant future,

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:13.970
we won't have to worry
about a lot of these.

00:03:13.970 --> 00:03:17.040
But we've got billions and
billions of parameters,

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:20.040
they're not going to fit very
nicely into a single GPU.

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:22.790
So we have to be very respectful
of the memory constraints

00:03:22.790 --> 00:03:24.470
that we have.

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:27.480
So those are the realities
that we have to deal with.

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:29.210
And what are the
tools that we have

00:03:29.210 --> 00:03:31.320
to have to be able
to handle these?

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:35.480
Well, GPUs, I'm sure you've
noticed in the class cluster,

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:37.130
don't come in singletons.

00:03:37.130 --> 00:03:39.980
A single machine will
have multiple GPUs

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:43.020
within the same physical rack.

00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:44.330
And so here's an example.

00:03:44.330 --> 00:03:48.500
I took this, I think,
from the GPT Neo X paper.

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:51.140
But this is an old example,
but the same lesson

00:03:51.140 --> 00:03:55.000
applies to the H100 machines
that you have in class.

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:57.490
So here, there's
eight different GPUs.

00:03:57.490 --> 00:04:02.250
They're connected to the various
CPUs through fast interconnects.

00:04:02.250 --> 00:04:05.380
Within each GPUs, you see this
NVSwitch thing at the bottom.

00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:07.170
This is very, very
fast connections

00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:08.950
across these eight GPUs.

00:04:08.950 --> 00:04:11.760
But if these eight GPUs
want to talk to GPUs

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:13.320
on a different
machine, they're going

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:15.610
to have to go through
a networking switch.

00:04:15.610 --> 00:04:18.370
And you see this purple line
that says, HDR InfiniBand.

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:21.390
That's a much slower
connection compared

00:04:21.390 --> 00:04:22.710
to the NVLink connection.

00:04:22.710 --> 00:04:24.910
You can see the difference
in the throughput

00:04:24.910 --> 00:04:28.260
that's about eight
times slower per lane.

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:30.900
And so this hardware
hierarchy that we

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:33.342
have is going to have
big implications for how

00:04:33.342 --> 00:04:34.800
we're going to end
up parallelizing

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:36.280
our models in practice.

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:39.300
And so you can keep this
mental model with you

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:40.720
as I talk through these things.

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:43.110
We have very, very
fast connections

00:04:43.110 --> 00:04:44.380
within a single machine.

00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:46.270
And then when we
go across machines,

00:04:46.270 --> 00:04:47.350
it's going to get slower.

00:04:47.350 --> 00:04:50.770
And then depending on the
kind of hardware we're using,

00:04:50.770 --> 00:04:53.300
there might even be
another level of slowness

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:58.840
once we go beyond, let's say,
256 GPUs networked together.

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:01.150
Many of you may
already know this

00:05:01.150 --> 00:05:03.350
having taken systems
or networking classes,

00:05:03.350 --> 00:05:05.740
but here's a very,
very brief refresher

00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:08.635
on collective
communication operations.

00:05:08.635 --> 00:05:10.510
And the reason why I'm
going to bring this up

00:05:10.510 --> 00:05:13.690
is there's one particular
important identity

00:05:13.690 --> 00:05:15.850
or equivalence
that you will need

00:05:15.850 --> 00:05:18.130
to know to really
understand some of the finer

00:05:18.130 --> 00:05:20.260
points of the performance
characteristics

00:05:20.260 --> 00:05:22.070
of the parallelization
algorithms.

00:05:22.070 --> 00:05:24.490
So I'll talk through
these, and then I'll

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:27.980
talk through one important
performance implication.

00:05:27.980 --> 00:05:30.940
So the first one, which all
of you probably have heard of,

00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:31.820
is all reduce.

00:05:31.820 --> 00:05:34.330
So you have four
machines, four ranks

00:05:34.330 --> 00:05:37.360
in this case, each one
having its own piece of data.

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:38.860
And what you'd like
to do is perform

00:05:38.860 --> 00:05:40.130
some of reduction operation.

00:05:40.130 --> 00:05:42.590
Let's say, I want to
sum all of these inputs,

00:05:42.590 --> 00:05:45.310
and then I want the
output to be copied over

00:05:45.310 --> 00:05:47.662
to every single machine.

00:05:47.662 --> 00:05:50.230
And this is going to
have roughly the cost

00:05:50.230 --> 00:05:55.010
of two times the total number of
things that you're all reducing.

00:05:55.010 --> 00:05:56.790
You have a broadcast operation.

00:05:56.790 --> 00:05:59.940
And here, I'm taking a
single input from rank two,

00:05:59.940 --> 00:06:02.670
and I'd like to copy it out
to all of the remaining ranks.

00:06:02.670 --> 00:06:04.190
And this is going
to have roughly

00:06:04.190 --> 00:06:08.330
on the order of one times
the total number of outputs

00:06:08.330 --> 00:06:10.020
in terms of the
communication costs.

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:12.592
And then we've got reduction
where we got different inputs,

00:06:12.592 --> 00:06:14.300
and that's going to
be summed up and then

00:06:14.300 --> 00:06:15.750
sent only to one machine.

00:06:15.750 --> 00:06:18.860
And then the two that are quite
important, even though these

00:06:18.860 --> 00:06:21.650
may not be quite as common, is
going to be the all gathering

00:06:21.650 --> 00:06:22.200
scatter.

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:24.680
So all gather is
an operation where

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:28.320
I'm taking a single
subcomponent of, let's say,

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:30.890
my parameters from rank
zero and I'm copying it

00:06:30.890 --> 00:06:32.720
over to all the ranks.

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:34.043
Same thing with rank 1, 2, 3.

00:06:34.043 --> 00:06:35.960
So each of these are
handling different parts,

00:06:35.960 --> 00:06:38.030
let's say, the parameters
and they're copied over

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:39.197
to the rest of the machines.

00:06:39.197 --> 00:06:42.140
So that's copying what
I have to everyone else

00:06:42.140 --> 00:06:47.730
and then reduce scatter, which
is I'm taking each of the rows,

00:06:47.730 --> 00:06:49.490
let's say, I'm summing
them up, and then

00:06:49.490 --> 00:06:52.360
I'm sending the result
only to rank zero.

00:06:52.360 --> 00:06:55.000
So this is a partial
version of an all reduce.

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:57.270
And hopefully, this
diagram makes it clear

00:06:57.270 --> 00:06:59.650
how reduce scatter works.

00:06:59.650 --> 00:07:01.560
And so all gather
and reduce scatter

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:04.720
are quite important
because in some sense,

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:07.860
they are the primitive by which
many of the parallelization

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:09.340
algorithms are
going to be built.

00:07:09.340 --> 00:07:13.780
And so this is an important
equivalence or an identity.

00:07:13.780 --> 00:07:16.200
I will refer to it
one or two times

00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:18.160
as key points in this lecture.

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:20.250
If you want to do an
all reduce, let's say,

00:07:20.250 --> 00:07:25.410
I've got different GPUs, A,
B, C, D, and each of the GPUs

00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:27.100
are handling a
different data point.

00:07:27.100 --> 00:07:28.717
And so I've got
different gradients

00:07:28.717 --> 00:07:30.300
for each of these
data points, and I'm

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:31.900
going to need to
sum those gradients,

00:07:31.900 --> 00:07:33.817
and then I need to pass
all of those gradients

00:07:33.817 --> 00:07:34.660
back to the GPUs.

00:07:34.660 --> 00:07:37.020
This is a classic data
parallel operation

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:39.340
that I might need to
do across my four GPUs.

00:07:39.340 --> 00:07:41.130
So that would be an all reduce.

00:07:41.130 --> 00:07:42.660
One important thing,
though, is this

00:07:42.660 --> 00:07:45.810
could be replaced
with two operations,

00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:47.610
a reduce scatter and
all gather, where

00:07:47.610 --> 00:07:52.090
a reduce reduced scatter is
going to some each of the rows

00:07:52.090 --> 00:07:54.670
and then leave the result
of the rows in, let's say,

00:07:54.670 --> 00:07:56.900
GPU 0, 1, 2, 3 respectively.

00:07:56.900 --> 00:08:00.880
And then I'm going to do an
all gather to copy those back

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:03.040
out to the remaining GPUs.

00:08:03.040 --> 00:08:06.190
So each GPU now is
getting a full sum

00:08:06.190 --> 00:08:08.830
of a part of the
parameters, and then it's

00:08:08.830 --> 00:08:12.640
going to copy it back to
the remaining workers.

00:08:12.640 --> 00:08:14.870
And in the bandwidth
limited regime,

00:08:14.870 --> 00:08:16.820
this is basically the
best that you can do.

00:08:16.820 --> 00:08:18.340
All reduce, the
best that you can do

00:08:18.340 --> 00:08:21.207
is roughly matching
the bandwidth

00:08:21.207 --> 00:08:23.540
that you can get out of a
reduce scatter and all gather.

00:08:23.540 --> 00:08:25.707
And you can convince yourself
of this by writing out

00:08:25.707 --> 00:08:28.360
how many communication
operations happen in both all

00:08:28.360 --> 00:08:30.195
reduce and the right hand side.

00:08:32.980 --> 00:08:36.039
The final thing that I
want to briefly touch on

00:08:36.039 --> 00:08:38.740
before I move on to talking
about the parallelization

00:08:38.740 --> 00:08:40.600
algorithms, and this
is the one place

00:08:40.600 --> 00:08:42.820
I'll talk about GPU versus TPU.

00:08:42.820 --> 00:08:45.520
Most of the discussion today
can actually abstract out

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:48.600
the underlying hardware,
but there is actually

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:50.730
one important thing that
I'll mention up front,

00:08:50.730 --> 00:08:54.600
so that I can refer to it
later as I talk through this.

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:57.120
How do we network together
different machines

00:08:57.120 --> 00:09:00.130
or different
accelerators in GPUs?

00:09:00.130 --> 00:09:04.000
Well, as I showed you in
the GPT Neo X slide here,

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:06.060
how in the GPU
world this generally

00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:09.503
works is you've got nodes,
single machines that contain,

00:09:09.503 --> 00:09:10.920
let's say, 8 GPUs,
and then you've

00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:15.580
got these switches that connect
fairly quickly to each other.

00:09:15.580 --> 00:09:20.560
And these machines are connected
all to all up to about 256 GPUs.

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:22.620
So that's an
important threshold up

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:25.470
until which you have very
fast, arbitrary communication

00:09:25.470 --> 00:09:26.650
between machines.

00:09:26.650 --> 00:09:29.070
And then above that,
you're actually

00:09:29.070 --> 00:09:33.330
going to need much more slow
communication, these leaf

00:09:33.330 --> 00:09:36.570
switches and spine switches,
once you go beyond roughly

00:09:36.570 --> 00:09:38.680
a single rack's worth of GPU.

00:09:38.680 --> 00:09:42.670
On the other hand, if you look
at TPU design from Google,

00:09:42.670 --> 00:09:44.550
they actually take a
very different approach

00:09:44.550 --> 00:09:47.420
to networking their machines.

00:09:47.420 --> 00:09:50.080
You've got a single
TPU chip, and they all

00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:52.910
talk to their neighbors
very, very quickly.

00:09:52.910 --> 00:09:56.380
And so this is a very easily
expandable what they call

00:09:56.380 --> 00:09:57.380
toroidal mesh.

00:09:57.380 --> 00:10:00.212
But you can only talk
to your neighbors.

00:10:00.212 --> 00:10:02.170
And the reason why I'm
talking about this right

00:10:02.170 --> 00:10:05.022
after the all reduce slide
is if you think about doing

00:10:05.022 --> 00:10:06.730
these kinds of collective
communications,

00:10:06.730 --> 00:10:09.190
like allreduce or
reduce scatter,

00:10:09.190 --> 00:10:11.980
you can implement them just as
efficiently on a toroidal mesh

00:10:11.980 --> 00:10:14.470
than you can on all
to all connection.

00:10:14.470 --> 00:10:16.030
And so if you're
optimizing purely

00:10:16.030 --> 00:10:17.860
for collective
communications, it

00:10:17.860 --> 00:10:20.530
makes sense to think about
things like TPU networking

00:10:20.530 --> 00:10:21.890
rather than GPU networking.

00:10:21.890 --> 00:10:23.980
I'll talk a little bit
about pros and cons of this

00:10:23.980 --> 00:10:29.560
later as I go through different
parallelization operations.

00:10:29.560 --> 00:10:31.900
So just to put this
together, now we're

00:10:31.900 --> 00:10:34.730
going to start talking
about a new unit of compute.

00:10:34.730 --> 00:10:37.153
Instead of the GPU, the new
unit is the data center.

00:10:37.153 --> 00:10:39.070
The whole data center
is going to be the thing

00:10:39.070 --> 00:10:40.690
that we're going to be doing.

00:10:40.690 --> 00:10:43.430
And now, we're going to try
to come up with algorithms

00:10:43.430 --> 00:10:46.860
and sharding strategies that
get us two different things.

00:10:46.860 --> 00:10:49.560
The first one is
linear memory scaling.

00:10:49.560 --> 00:10:52.080
So as I scale up
the number of GPUs,

00:10:52.080 --> 00:10:54.140
the biggest model
that I can train

00:10:54.140 --> 00:10:55.880
is going to scale
linearly with that.

00:10:55.880 --> 00:10:58.380
So I can train bigger and bigger
models if I really want to.

00:10:58.380 --> 00:11:01.100
I also want linear
compute scaling.

00:11:01.100 --> 00:11:03.740
As I get more and
more GPUs, the useful

00:11:03.740 --> 00:11:07.920
computation that I'm doing to
train the model scales linearly.

00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:11.180
And then finally, a lot
of this, these algorithms

00:11:11.180 --> 00:11:13.168
are going to be
implemented by just calling

00:11:13.168 --> 00:11:14.960
these very simple
collective communications

00:11:14.960 --> 00:11:16.620
primitives in various ways.

00:11:16.620 --> 00:11:19.250
And so when we think about the
performance characteristics

00:11:19.250 --> 00:11:22.700
of these parallel
algorithms, it suffices

00:11:22.700 --> 00:11:24.710
to reason about
basically counting

00:11:24.710 --> 00:11:26.460
the collective
communications primitives.

00:11:26.460 --> 00:11:28.793
So that's kind of an important
way to think about these.

00:11:28.793 --> 00:11:31.850
We don't go all the way down
to the low level implementation

00:11:31.850 --> 00:11:34.446
of these algorithms here.

00:11:34.446 --> 00:11:36.390
And questions on part one?

00:11:36.390 --> 00:11:37.010
Yes.

00:11:37.010 --> 00:11:39.427
Sorry, but from
the previous slide,

00:11:39.427 --> 00:11:41.510
does it mean that it's
better to do reduce scatter

00:11:41.510 --> 00:11:44.300
as all gather rather than
the algorithms or algorithm?

00:11:44.300 --> 00:11:44.800
Right.

00:11:44.800 --> 00:11:45.820
So this slide, right?

00:11:45.820 --> 00:11:46.090
Yeah.

00:11:46.090 --> 00:11:48.548
So the conclusion of this slide
is that they're equivalent.

00:11:48.548 --> 00:11:52.950
And I think if you think
about something like doing

00:11:52.950 --> 00:11:55.560
gradient descent in
parallel, all reduce

00:11:55.560 --> 00:11:58.620
is a very natural operation
to do because you'll scatter

00:11:58.620 --> 00:12:02.200
your-- sorry, you'll distribute
your data to different machines.

00:12:02.200 --> 00:12:05.092
And then you'll have to all
reduce your gradients together.

00:12:05.092 --> 00:12:07.500
But what I'm saying is
this very natural thing

00:12:07.500 --> 00:12:09.540
to do of all reduce
can actually be written

00:12:09.540 --> 00:12:11.802
as a sum of two
different operations

00:12:11.802 --> 00:12:12.760
and they're equivalent.

00:12:12.760 --> 00:12:15.745
So there's no
performance hit by going

00:12:15.745 --> 00:12:18.120
from this left representation
to this right one, at least

00:12:18.120 --> 00:12:18.940
in bandwidth.

00:12:18.940 --> 00:12:22.080
And that's going to have
important implications in maybe

00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:23.110
five slides.

00:12:23.110 --> 00:12:26.970
So you can wait a little bit
to see why I mentioned this.

00:12:26.970 --> 00:12:29.140
OK.

00:12:29.140 --> 00:12:30.750
Any other questions?

00:12:30.750 --> 00:12:31.590
Good.

00:12:31.590 --> 00:12:33.570
OK.

00:12:33.570 --> 00:12:35.220
So now, we're going
to get started.

00:12:35.220 --> 00:12:39.330
In some sense, this is the
exciting algorithmic meat

00:12:39.330 --> 00:12:40.520
of the lecture.

00:12:40.520 --> 00:12:45.172
And there are three kinds
of parallelism strategies,

00:12:45.172 --> 00:12:47.630
parallelism things that we
should really be thinking about.

00:12:47.630 --> 00:12:50.240
So the first one is
data parallelism.

00:12:50.240 --> 00:12:54.130
So data parallelism at a
high level is the idea of I'm

00:12:54.130 --> 00:12:56.260
going to roughly
copy the parameters

00:12:56.260 --> 00:12:57.860
across my different GPUs.

00:12:57.860 --> 00:13:00.290
I'm not going to worry about
splitting my parameters up,

00:13:00.290 --> 00:13:03.460
but I will take my batch,
and I will split my batch up.

00:13:03.460 --> 00:13:05.440
And different GPUs
or different machines

00:13:05.440 --> 00:13:08.600
will get different
slices of my batch.

00:13:08.600 --> 00:13:10.040
So that's data parallelism.

00:13:10.040 --> 00:13:13.000
There's lots of subtleties
in how we execute that.

00:13:13.000 --> 00:13:15.430
Model parallelism
now is starting

00:13:15.430 --> 00:13:17.710
to say, OK, I don't
want all my GPUs

00:13:17.710 --> 00:13:19.850
to have all the different
parts of my model.

00:13:19.850 --> 00:13:22.880
As my models get bigger, that's
going to be a very big problem.

00:13:22.880 --> 00:13:25.340
So I need to cut up my
model in very clever ways,

00:13:25.340 --> 00:13:28.040
and I need my GPU to handle
different parts of my model.

00:13:28.040 --> 00:13:30.430
So that's going to
be model parallelism.

00:13:30.430 --> 00:13:34.690
And then the final piece
is activation parallelism.

00:13:34.690 --> 00:13:37.540
We don't really think too much
about activations in our day

00:13:37.540 --> 00:13:40.310
to day lives lies because
the PyTorch handles it

00:13:40.310 --> 00:13:41.730
very transparently.

00:13:41.730 --> 00:13:46.310
But as the models get bigger and
the sequence lengths get longer,

00:13:46.310 --> 00:13:49.602
the activation memory starts
to be a really big problem.

00:13:49.602 --> 00:13:51.560
So if you want to train
these really big models

00:13:51.560 --> 00:13:53.720
with big, big batch
sizes, you have

00:13:53.720 --> 00:13:56.970
to somehow manage the memory
footprint of your activations.

00:13:56.970 --> 00:13:58.590
And so we have to
split those up too.

00:13:58.590 --> 00:14:00.830
So there's some
ways to handle that.

00:14:00.830 --> 00:14:02.610
And when we put
all these together,

00:14:02.610 --> 00:14:04.790
we will have all the
tools we need in order

00:14:04.790 --> 00:14:07.790
to scale up both compute
and memory gracefully as we

00:14:07.790 --> 00:14:09.740
have lots and lots of machines.

00:14:09.740 --> 00:14:12.823
So these are the core
conceptual objects.

00:14:12.823 --> 00:14:14.990
And now, we're going to
talk about implementing each

00:14:14.990 --> 00:14:18.620
of these ideas efficiently.

00:14:18.620 --> 00:14:23.280
So the starting point of
data parallelism is just SGD.

00:14:23.280 --> 00:14:26.910
If we're doing very naive batch
stochastic gradient descent,

00:14:26.910 --> 00:14:30.260
the formula for doing this
looks like this equation

00:14:30.260 --> 00:14:33.620
that I have right here
on the slide right here.

00:14:33.620 --> 00:14:36.060
I'm taking a batch
size capital B,

00:14:36.060 --> 00:14:37.920
and I'm going to sum
up all those gradients,

00:14:37.920 --> 00:14:39.880
and I'm going to
update my parameters.

00:14:39.880 --> 00:14:43.260
So naive data parallelism
is just saying, all right,

00:14:43.260 --> 00:14:46.560
take your batch size B,
split that up, and send that

00:14:46.560 --> 00:14:47.680
to different machines.

00:14:47.680 --> 00:14:51.070
Each machine will compute
some part of the sum.

00:14:51.070 --> 00:14:53.250
And then I will exchange
all of my gradients

00:14:53.250 --> 00:14:55.980
together to synchronize
after each--

00:14:55.980 --> 00:14:58.500
before each gradient step, I
will synchronize my gradients,

00:14:58.500 --> 00:15:01.272
and then I will take
a parameter update.

00:15:01.272 --> 00:15:04.142
So now, I've been talking to
you about compute and memory

00:15:04.142 --> 00:15:05.350
scaling and all these things.

00:15:05.350 --> 00:15:09.310
So let's just talk through what
it looks like for each of these.

00:15:09.310 --> 00:15:13.650
So for compute scaling, data
parallelism is pretty great.

00:15:13.650 --> 00:15:17.530
Each machine, each GPU is
going to get B over M examples.

00:15:17.530 --> 00:15:19.380
And if my batch
size is big enough,

00:15:19.380 --> 00:15:22.380
each GPU is going to get
a pretty decent batch

00:15:22.380 --> 00:15:24.360
size, micro batch size.

00:15:24.360 --> 00:15:27.160
And it's able to hopefully
saturate its compute.

00:15:27.160 --> 00:15:28.650
So that's good.

00:15:28.650 --> 00:15:30.640
What's the
communication overhead?

00:15:30.640 --> 00:15:32.310
Well, I'm going to
have to transmit

00:15:32.310 --> 00:15:35.870
twice the number of my
parameters, every batch.

00:15:35.870 --> 00:15:38.050
Remember, on all reduce
is going to roughly

00:15:38.050 --> 00:15:40.540
be twice the amount of stuff
that you're all reducing

00:15:40.540 --> 00:15:42.850
in terms of communication cost.

00:15:42.850 --> 00:15:45.350
And so this is OK if
the batch size is big.

00:15:45.350 --> 00:15:46.880
If my batch sizes
are really big,

00:15:46.880 --> 00:15:49.120
I can mask the
communication overhead

00:15:49.120 --> 00:15:53.050
of having to synchronize my
gradients every now and then.

00:15:53.050 --> 00:15:55.910
Memory scaling, I'm not
touching this at all right.

00:15:55.910 --> 00:15:58.970
Every GPU needs to replicate
the number of parameters.

00:15:58.970 --> 00:16:01.190
It needs to replicate
the optimizer state.

00:16:01.190 --> 00:16:04.060
It's pretty bad
for memory scaling.

00:16:04.060 --> 00:16:07.780
So if we didn't have to
worry about memory at all,

00:16:07.780 --> 00:16:11.050
this is an OK strategy.

00:16:11.050 --> 00:16:14.780
But I think in practice
memory is a problem.

00:16:14.780 --> 00:16:16.510
I think every one
of you sitting here

00:16:16.510 --> 00:16:19.630
has experienced trying to
put a big model onto a GPU

00:16:19.630 --> 00:16:22.240
and PyTorch telling you,
oh, you're out of memory.

00:16:22.240 --> 00:16:25.090
And this is really a
problem with your training

00:16:25.090 --> 00:16:28.430
as well because if you can
fit more and more batch sizes,

00:16:28.430 --> 00:16:31.910
that's going to make the
data parallel more efficient.

00:16:31.910 --> 00:16:35.180
And so ideally, you'd
like to save on memory.

00:16:35.180 --> 00:16:38.880
So let's take a closer look at
the memory usage of naive data

00:16:38.880 --> 00:16:40.790
parallel.

00:16:40.790 --> 00:16:44.280
And the memory situation is
actually worse than it looks.

00:16:44.280 --> 00:16:47.930
It's actually quite
terrible, because you've

00:16:47.930 --> 00:16:50.780
done this in
assignment one, but we

00:16:50.780 --> 00:16:55.080
can think about how many copies
of our model we need to store.

00:16:55.080 --> 00:16:56.212
And it's very large.

00:16:56.212 --> 00:16:57.920
Depending on the
precision by which we're

00:16:57.920 --> 00:17:00.260
doing some of our
training, you're

00:17:00.260 --> 00:17:03.740
going to need to store
something like 16 bytes of data

00:17:03.740 --> 00:17:04.473
per parameter.

00:17:04.473 --> 00:17:06.140
And in fact, you need
to store something

00:17:06.140 --> 00:17:08.540
like five copies
of your weights.

00:17:08.540 --> 00:17:11.040
And this is really quite bad.

00:17:11.040 --> 00:17:13.130
Because if you just want
to think about your model

00:17:13.130 --> 00:17:15.589
parameters, technically
you only need two bytes.

00:17:15.589 --> 00:17:17.849
So where did that
factor of 8 come from?

00:17:17.849 --> 00:17:19.829
Well, at least,
you need gradients.

00:17:19.829 --> 00:17:21.920
And if you're computing
your gradients in BF16,

00:17:21.920 --> 00:17:23.310
that's another 2 bytes.

00:17:23.310 --> 00:17:25.710
But then your optimizer
state shows up,

00:17:25.710 --> 00:17:28.220
and that's a really big
problem, because you've

00:17:28.220 --> 00:17:31.010
got 4 bytes of master
weights, the things

00:17:31.010 --> 00:17:32.625
that you're
accumulating into SGD,

00:17:32.625 --> 00:17:35.700
like these intermediate
sums that you're doing.

00:17:35.700 --> 00:17:39.700
You need 4 or 2 bytes for
Adam's first moment estimates,

00:17:39.700 --> 00:17:42.610
because remember Adam keeps
track of historical gradients.

00:17:42.610 --> 00:17:45.120
And then Adam also needs
second moment estimates,

00:17:45.120 --> 00:17:48.610
the variance of the gradients
that you've gotten in the past.

00:17:48.610 --> 00:17:51.460
And that's going to need
another 4 or 2 bytes.

00:17:51.460 --> 00:17:54.840
And so what originally
looked fine is actually now

00:17:54.840 --> 00:17:57.090
looking quite grim.

00:17:57.090 --> 00:18:02.580
And so the 16X, if I just
draw it as a picture,

00:18:02.580 --> 00:18:05.170
you realize that most
of your memory usage,

00:18:05.170 --> 00:18:07.450
at least in terms
of parameter memory,

00:18:07.450 --> 00:18:12.300
is really being dominated by the
optimizer states of your Adam

00:18:12.300 --> 00:18:13.150
optimizer.

00:18:13.150 --> 00:18:16.110
So your memory
consumed is going to be

00:18:16.110 --> 00:18:20.990
a function of how many bytes are
being used for your optimizer

00:18:20.990 --> 00:18:21.490
state.

00:18:21.490 --> 00:18:25.410
And that's generally going
to be even more than the core

00:18:25.410 --> 00:18:27.610
parameter and
gradient memory usage.

00:18:27.610 --> 00:18:33.220
And so for a simple example of
a 7.5B model distributed over 64

00:18:33.220 --> 00:18:36.250
accelerators, you're
using a ton of memory.

00:18:36.250 --> 00:18:39.340
And this memory scales
linearly upwards, total memory

00:18:39.340 --> 00:18:42.680
at least scales linearly
upwards with the number of GPUs.

00:18:42.680 --> 00:18:45.160
So that's no good at all.

00:18:45.160 --> 00:18:47.510
But if once we look
at this picture,

00:18:47.510 --> 00:18:49.730
we get some very simple ideas.

00:18:49.730 --> 00:18:53.540
You might wonder clearly
or maybe not clearly,

00:18:53.540 --> 00:18:55.600
I need the parameters
and gradients

00:18:55.600 --> 00:18:57.310
to be copied across devices.

00:18:57.310 --> 00:19:00.140
That seems necessary
to do data parallel.

00:19:00.140 --> 00:19:02.800
But do I really need
all the optimizer states

00:19:02.800 --> 00:19:04.700
to be on every single machine?

00:19:04.700 --> 00:19:07.090
And once you ask
that question, you

00:19:07.090 --> 00:19:09.080
can maybe get to
the second row here.

00:19:09.080 --> 00:19:13.010
And this is going to be called
optimizer state sharding.

00:19:13.010 --> 00:19:15.410
And if we could do that,
then at least in this case,

00:19:15.410 --> 00:19:21.130
we can go from 120 gigabytes of
total memory usage down to 31.4.

00:19:21.130 --> 00:19:23.780
And then maybe we can start
sharding the gradients,

00:19:23.780 --> 00:19:27.020
and then now, we can get to
16.6 gigabytes of memory usage.

00:19:27.020 --> 00:19:29.220
And then if we also
shard the parameters,

00:19:29.220 --> 00:19:32.700
we can go all the way down to
1.9 gigabytes of memory usage.

00:19:32.700 --> 00:19:34.710
And that would be a
pretty good place to be,

00:19:34.710 --> 00:19:37.160
because now, we've
fully sharded out

00:19:37.160 --> 00:19:40.220
all the optimizer state and
parameter and gradient memory

00:19:40.220 --> 00:19:40.850
that we need.

00:19:40.850 --> 00:19:41.630
Yes.

00:19:41.630 --> 00:19:44.690
So why would we shard
the optimizer state

00:19:44.690 --> 00:19:49.310
if we're doing, I guess,
the gradient computation

00:19:49.310 --> 00:19:52.640
on each of them and then
[INAUDIBLE] reducing that?

00:19:52.640 --> 00:19:55.642
Then how can we
have a [INAUDIBLE]?

00:19:55.642 --> 00:19:56.850
That is a very good question.

00:19:56.850 --> 00:19:59.480
And the question is, how can
we shard the optimizer state?

00:19:59.480 --> 00:20:02.300
When we're doing
data parallel, GPU 0

00:20:02.300 --> 00:20:04.730
has to be responsible
for data point 1.

00:20:04.730 --> 00:20:07.490
So clearly, it needs to know
about all the parameters

00:20:07.490 --> 00:20:08.130
and update it.

00:20:08.130 --> 00:20:11.210
So how can I possibly
shard the optimizer state?

00:20:11.210 --> 00:20:13.100
And in a way, I
think, zero, which

00:20:13.100 --> 00:20:15.020
is what this is,
this is the zero

00:20:15.020 --> 00:20:18.140
overhead data
parallel optimizer,

00:20:18.140 --> 00:20:20.330
this is a very, in
some ways, clever idea.

00:20:20.330 --> 00:20:23.430
Because it shows you that even
when you're doing data parallel,

00:20:23.430 --> 00:20:26.600
you don't actually need to copy
everything onto every machine.

00:20:26.600 --> 00:20:29.940
You can be really clever about
how you do communications

00:20:29.940 --> 00:20:31.180
to avoid all of this.

00:20:31.180 --> 00:20:33.430
So I will talk
through exactly this.

00:20:33.430 --> 00:20:35.753
This is a great question.

00:20:35.753 --> 00:20:37.170
So what we're going
to do is we're

00:20:37.170 --> 00:20:39.253
going to split up the
optimizer states, as I said.

00:20:39.253 --> 00:20:41.100
So the first and
second moments are now

00:20:41.100 --> 00:20:43.620
split up across all the GPUs.

00:20:43.620 --> 00:20:45.820
But everyone has the
parameters and the gradients.

00:20:45.820 --> 00:20:47.440
So why is this important?

00:20:47.440 --> 00:20:49.410
If I have the parameters
and gradients,

00:20:49.410 --> 00:20:52.140
let's say, I'm GPU zero, I have
the parameters and gradients

00:20:52.140 --> 00:20:53.170
for everything.

00:20:53.170 --> 00:20:57.060
That's enough information for
me to compute the full gradient.

00:20:57.060 --> 00:21:00.190
The full gradient update for
this example can be computed.

00:21:00.190 --> 00:21:03.750
The only thing I can't do is
I can't take that gradient

00:21:03.750 --> 00:21:04.810
and take an atom step.

00:21:04.810 --> 00:21:07.410
I can't update my
parameters unless I see

00:21:07.410 --> 00:21:09.120
all of the optimizer states.

00:21:09.120 --> 00:21:11.140
So that's the key idea.

00:21:11.140 --> 00:21:13.260
And so now, what's
going to happen

00:21:13.260 --> 00:21:17.470
is GPU 0 is going to compute
the gradients for everything,

00:21:17.470 --> 00:21:20.520
but GPU 0 is now only
responsible for updating

00:21:20.520 --> 00:21:23.140
the parameters for the
shard that they own.

00:21:23.140 --> 00:21:25.060
And that's the key idea.

00:21:25.060 --> 00:21:28.100
We're going to distribute the
work of updating the parameters.

00:21:28.100 --> 00:21:30.590
And then we're going to
synchronize the parameters back.

00:21:30.590 --> 00:21:35.150
So let me show you in much
more gory detail how this works

00:21:35.150 --> 00:21:38.590
and the reason why
it's called 0 overhead.

00:21:38.590 --> 00:21:44.150
So step one, every GPU gets a
different data point let's say.

00:21:44.150 --> 00:21:46.400
I'm just going to simplify
all this batch computation.

00:21:46.400 --> 00:21:48.320
I have GPU 0 through,
let's say, 4.

00:21:48.320 --> 00:21:50.630
And every GPU gets
a single example,

00:21:50.630 --> 00:21:54.020
and they compute a full gradient
on the example that they own.

00:21:54.020 --> 00:21:56.500
Now, what I'm
going to do next is

00:21:56.500 --> 00:21:59.150
I'm going to reduce
scatter the gradients.

00:21:59.150 --> 00:22:02.386
So I'm going to send
the gradients that--

00:22:02.386 --> 00:22:04.030
I'm going to collect
in some sense

00:22:04.030 --> 00:22:06.260
the gradients that
each GPU owns.

00:22:06.260 --> 00:22:08.680
So GPU 0, let's
say, is responsible

00:22:08.680 --> 00:22:11.120
for this first quarter
of the parameters.

00:22:11.120 --> 00:22:13.750
So the parameters
are the Y-axis here

00:22:13.750 --> 00:22:15.737
and the X-axis here is GPUs.

00:22:15.737 --> 00:22:17.320
And so what we're
going to do is we're

00:22:17.320 --> 00:22:19.300
going to reduce
scatter to make sure

00:22:19.300 --> 00:22:22.900
that GPU 0 has all of
the gradient information

00:22:22.900 --> 00:22:25.880
from all of the other GPUs
for the subset of parameters

00:22:25.880 --> 00:22:27.320
that it is responsible for.

00:22:27.320 --> 00:22:29.960
So now, it gets this
gradient information

00:22:29.960 --> 00:22:35.010
from GPU1 and GPU2 and GPU3, and
that's all reduced into GPU 0.

00:22:35.010 --> 00:22:37.620
Hopefully, that's clear.

00:22:37.620 --> 00:22:40.070
Now, GPU 0 has all
the information

00:22:40.070 --> 00:22:42.540
it needs to update
its own parameters,

00:22:42.540 --> 00:22:45.080
because it has the optimizer
state corresponding

00:22:45.080 --> 00:22:46.140
to this first part.

00:22:46.140 --> 00:22:49.470
It has a full summed
gradient for this first part.

00:22:49.470 --> 00:22:51.890
And now, so it's going
to take a gradient update

00:22:51.890 --> 00:22:55.410
on their part of the parameters
using gradient and state.

00:22:55.410 --> 00:22:58.160
And so that now I
have the full updated

00:22:58.160 --> 00:23:02.600
parameters for this subset in
my GPU 0, and all I need to do

00:23:02.600 --> 00:23:06.590
is all gather all of the
updated parameters back

00:23:06.590 --> 00:23:08.310
in to all the ranks.

00:23:08.310 --> 00:23:10.013
So there's many questions here.

00:23:10.013 --> 00:23:10.680
I'll start here.

00:23:10.680 --> 00:23:11.577
Yes.

00:23:11.577 --> 00:23:13.680
Would you say [INAUDIBLE]
the number of parameters

00:23:13.680 --> 00:23:15.330
that's permission, right?

00:23:15.330 --> 00:23:17.210
Or is that [INAUDIBLE].

00:23:17.210 --> 00:23:17.900
Sorry.

00:23:17.900 --> 00:23:19.108
Say that again. and speak up.

00:23:19.108 --> 00:23:20.997
[INAUDIBLE] the
number of parameters,

00:23:20.997 --> 00:23:22.080
that's per machine, right?

00:23:22.080 --> 00:23:23.700
Or is that total?

00:23:23.700 --> 00:23:26.520
So the question was whether the
number of params communication

00:23:26.520 --> 00:23:28.800
cost was per machine
or its total.

00:23:28.800 --> 00:23:31.680
Here, it's going to
be total, because--

00:23:31.680 --> 00:23:34.950
so this is going to be 1/4 of
the parameter is going to be

00:23:34.950 --> 00:23:36.970
sent 3 times to this machine.

00:23:36.970 --> 00:23:39.540
And then you repeat
that 4 times.

00:23:39.540 --> 00:23:41.280
In slide one, you
said it was two times

00:23:41.280 --> 00:23:42.330
the number of parameters.

00:23:42.330 --> 00:23:44.130
Was that per machine or--

00:23:44.130 --> 00:23:45.280
That was also total.

00:23:45.280 --> 00:23:45.395
Oh.

00:23:45.395 --> 00:23:45.510
OK.

00:23:45.510 --> 00:23:45.720
Yeah.

00:23:45.720 --> 00:23:47.350
2 times number of
parameters is total,

00:23:47.350 --> 00:23:50.100
because each block is
going to have to be sent

00:23:50.100 --> 00:23:52.410
to every other kind of machine.

00:23:52.410 --> 00:23:54.730
OK.

00:23:54.730 --> 00:23:55.540
Yes.

00:23:55.540 --> 00:23:58.420
So this question is not unique
to what you're showing here,

00:23:58.420 --> 00:23:59.650
but you made me think of it.

00:23:59.650 --> 00:24:02.340
So the Adam optimizer
that you showed

00:24:02.340 --> 00:24:07.320
seems to assume, largely assume
independence of parameters.

00:24:07.320 --> 00:24:12.790
We've drawn all these diagrams
that show the opposite.

00:24:12.790 --> 00:24:15.850
We have connected
nodes and all that.

00:24:15.850 --> 00:24:17.310
And it seems,
especially pressing

00:24:17.310 --> 00:24:21.640
when we're trying to split these
and update them separately,

00:24:21.640 --> 00:24:23.030
does that create any issues?

00:24:23.030 --> 00:24:23.530
OK.

00:24:23.530 --> 00:24:26.020
So the question was Adam W
seems to assume parameters

00:24:26.020 --> 00:24:26.960
operate independently.

00:24:26.960 --> 00:24:30.550
I'm assuming because you're
saying, we track gradient sums

00:24:30.550 --> 00:24:32.810
and then we diagonally
update the parameters.

00:24:32.810 --> 00:24:36.590
But we know that that
that's not fully diagonal,

00:24:36.590 --> 00:24:37.930
and so is there a problem.

00:24:37.930 --> 00:24:42.100
There have been better
attempts at improving

00:24:42.100 --> 00:24:44.745
Adam W to not just be diagonal.

00:24:44.745 --> 00:24:46.120
There's things
like K-FAC and all

00:24:46.120 --> 00:24:48.940
these other second
order style optimizers

00:24:48.940 --> 00:24:50.410
that people have come up with.

00:24:50.410 --> 00:24:52.600
They haven't dethroned
Adam, even though they

00:24:52.600 --> 00:24:53.960
do have their advantages.

00:24:53.960 --> 00:24:55.710
And there's some really
interesting things

00:24:55.710 --> 00:24:58.270
that you can do with these
kinds of improved second order

00:24:58.270 --> 00:25:01.000
preconditioning methods.

00:25:01.000 --> 00:25:02.300
Yes.

00:25:02.300 --> 00:25:04.780
What was the rows that
we're reducing over?

00:25:04.780 --> 00:25:07.780
What is the rows that
we're reducing over?

00:25:07.780 --> 00:25:10.370
So you're asking, what is
the rows of this picture?

00:25:10.370 --> 00:25:10.870
Yeah.

00:25:10.870 --> 00:25:11.370
Yeah.

00:25:11.370 --> 00:25:14.810
So imagine this is
parameters here in the rows.

00:25:14.810 --> 00:25:18.290
So GPU 0 is responsible for
some number of parameters.

00:25:18.290 --> 00:25:20.750
So this is a block
of parameters up top.

00:25:20.750 --> 00:25:22.700
And so when we do
reduce scatter,

00:25:22.700 --> 00:25:26.000
we're saying, take the
gradients for example

00:25:26.000 --> 00:25:27.530
0 for this block of parameters.

00:25:27.530 --> 00:25:30.080
Take the gradients for
example 1 for this same block

00:25:30.080 --> 00:25:30.870
of parameters.

00:25:30.870 --> 00:25:33.480
And then sum them all
and put them in rank 0.

00:25:33.480 --> 00:25:36.600
That's what we're saying here.

00:25:36.600 --> 00:25:37.100
Cool.

00:25:37.100 --> 00:25:38.270
OK.

00:25:38.270 --> 00:25:41.930
And the key thing here
is we're doing a reduce

00:25:41.930 --> 00:25:43.530
scatter and an all gather.

00:25:43.530 --> 00:25:48.000
And if you remember what
I was saying before, well,

00:25:48.000 --> 00:25:49.940
a reduce scatter and
an all gather has

00:25:49.940 --> 00:25:51.720
the same cost as an all reduce.

00:25:51.720 --> 00:25:55.490
And so there is a little bit of
a surprising magic thing that

00:25:55.490 --> 00:25:57.860
happened here, which
is that, well, we

00:25:57.860 --> 00:26:00.413
were doing an all reduce
before on all the gradients

00:26:00.413 --> 00:26:02.580
to make sure everyone's
gradients were synchronized.

00:26:02.580 --> 00:26:04.950
And that cost us 2 times
the number of parameters.

00:26:04.950 --> 00:26:08.880
But if we're clever about
how we're doing the updates,

00:26:08.880 --> 00:26:11.490
well, we can do a reduce
scatter and an all gather,

00:26:11.490 --> 00:26:14.720
and in between the two steps,
we can do some computation.

00:26:14.720 --> 00:26:17.620
And that gives us the same
amount of compute communication

00:26:17.620 --> 00:26:19.910
cost but now, at least
for the optimizer state,

00:26:19.910 --> 00:26:23.180
we fully sharded the optimizer
state across the model.

00:26:23.180 --> 00:26:25.690
So 0 stage 1 is
in some sense free

00:26:25.690 --> 00:26:30.400
in the bandwidth limited regime
and gives you memory wins.

00:26:30.400 --> 00:26:32.710
Yes.

00:26:32.710 --> 00:26:35.350
[INAUDIBLE] can suppress
the memory contribution

00:26:35.350 --> 00:26:38.620
of the higher notes,
do people modify

00:26:38.620 --> 00:26:40.095
Adam to include higher moments?

00:26:43.360 --> 00:26:46.690
What do you mean by you can
suppress the higher order

00:26:46.690 --> 00:26:48.190
contributions?

00:26:48.190 --> 00:26:51.730
Right, so for first
and second moments,

00:26:51.730 --> 00:26:58.000
the amount of memory
per GPU is divided

00:26:58.000 --> 00:26:59.110
by the total [INAUDIBLE].

00:26:59.110 --> 00:26:59.840
Yes.

00:26:59.840 --> 00:27:00.520
That's right.

00:27:00.520 --> 00:27:03.820
So it seems like you might
as well shard [INAUDIBLE].

00:27:03.820 --> 00:27:04.340
I see.

00:27:04.340 --> 00:27:07.335
So you're roughly saying, you
could track way more optimizer

00:27:07.335 --> 00:27:08.960
state-- to rephrase
what you're saying,

00:27:08.960 --> 00:27:11.200
you could have even more
complicated optimizer state

00:27:11.200 --> 00:27:14.410
because you can divide
that by the number of GPUs.

00:27:14.410 --> 00:27:17.183
While this is true, what
we're going to do next

00:27:17.183 --> 00:27:19.350
is we're actually going to
make the other components

00:27:19.350 --> 00:27:20.647
scale with GPUs.

00:27:20.647 --> 00:27:23.230
So that's going to make things
in some sense not free anymore.

00:27:23.230 --> 00:27:25.587
Optimizer state will
continue to be the bottleneck

00:27:25.587 --> 00:27:27.670
if we can divide everything
by the number of GPUs.

00:27:27.670 --> 00:27:31.620
So hopefully that's a
reasonable convincing answer.

00:27:31.620 --> 00:27:34.740
So we're going to build up
stage by stage to ZeRO stage

00:27:34.740 --> 00:27:37.380
3, which is more complicated.

00:27:37.380 --> 00:27:40.090
ZeRO stage 2 is still
relatively simple.

00:27:40.090 --> 00:27:44.050
So now hopefully that optimizer
state sharding trick made sense.

00:27:44.050 --> 00:27:45.720
I think that's very cool.

00:27:45.720 --> 00:27:48.460
So now we want to
shard even more stuff.

00:27:48.460 --> 00:27:51.480
So I want to shard the
gradients across the machines.

00:27:51.480 --> 00:27:56.020
So roughly, we can do the same
kinds of trick as stage 1,

00:27:56.020 --> 00:27:59.010
but there is one
additional complexity.

00:27:59.010 --> 00:28:01.300
And so what's the
additional complexity?

00:28:01.300 --> 00:28:05.130
Well, we can never instantiate
a full gradient vector.

00:28:05.130 --> 00:28:07.270
If I ever do the
full backwards pass,

00:28:07.270 --> 00:28:10.260
and I try to compute a
full gradient vector,

00:28:10.260 --> 00:28:11.650
I might go out of memory.

00:28:11.650 --> 00:28:13.800
So I want my
maximum memory usage

00:28:13.800 --> 00:28:16.470
to basically be
bounded by this, which

00:28:16.470 --> 00:28:18.670
is like full parameters,
sharded gradient,

00:28:18.670 --> 00:28:20.140
sharded optimizer state.

00:28:20.140 --> 00:28:22.110
And so what we're
going to have to do

00:28:22.110 --> 00:28:24.430
is when we do the
backwards pass,

00:28:24.430 --> 00:28:26.290
as we're computing
the gradient vector,

00:28:26.290 --> 00:28:28.140
we can't instantiate
the full gradient first

00:28:28.140 --> 00:28:29.530
and then do communication.

00:28:29.530 --> 00:28:32.440
What we have to do is as we
compute the gradients backwards,

00:28:32.440 --> 00:28:34.780
as soon as we compute a
layer's worth of gradient,

00:28:34.780 --> 00:28:38.370
we're going to have to send that
over to the corresponding GPU

00:28:38.370 --> 00:28:40.650
that it belongs to.

00:28:40.650 --> 00:28:43.480
So this is how it works.

00:28:43.480 --> 00:28:44.800
It's roughly the same idea.

00:28:44.800 --> 00:28:48.340
So now everyone has their
own batch component.

00:28:48.340 --> 00:28:49.920
Everyone incrementally
goes backwards

00:28:49.920 --> 00:28:51.183
on the computation graph.

00:28:51.183 --> 00:28:53.350
And let's say we're going
to operate layer by layer.

00:28:53.350 --> 00:28:57.420
So layers are sharded
atomically to different GPUs.

00:28:57.420 --> 00:29:00.840
So what we're going to do
then is as we go backwards

00:29:00.840 --> 00:29:03.990
on the computation graph, after
we compute a layer's gradients,

00:29:03.990 --> 00:29:06.300
immediately call a reduction
operation to send this

00:29:06.300 --> 00:29:07.210
to the right worker.

00:29:07.210 --> 00:29:09.100
So a layer belongs
to some worker.

00:29:09.100 --> 00:29:11.200
Maybe it's like GPU
number 2 in this case.

00:29:11.200 --> 00:29:13.470
So we're just going to
immediately reduce that,

00:29:13.470 --> 00:29:16.730
send that to the
worker at that point.

00:29:16.730 --> 00:29:19.490
And gradients are
now no longer needed.

00:29:19.490 --> 00:29:22.370
I don't need to store the
gradients on rank 0, 1, and 3.

00:29:22.370 --> 00:29:24.350
So I can immediately free that.

00:29:24.350 --> 00:29:26.520
And then now we
continue this process.

00:29:26.520 --> 00:29:29.430
And so all the machines have
their fully updated gradients.

00:29:29.430 --> 00:29:31.280
And now they have
a full gradient

00:29:31.280 --> 00:29:32.820
for their share
of the parameters,

00:29:32.820 --> 00:29:34.820
they have a full optimizer
state for their share

00:29:34.820 --> 00:29:37.610
of the parameters, each machine
can update their parameters,

00:29:37.610 --> 00:29:41.075
and it all gather the
parameters back together.

00:29:41.075 --> 00:29:42.950
This looks like it's
maybe more communication

00:29:42.950 --> 00:29:46.550
because you're doing
this kind of reduction

00:29:46.550 --> 00:29:48.450
operation every layer.

00:29:48.450 --> 00:29:50.870
But this is only for a
small amount of parameters.

00:29:50.870 --> 00:29:51.630
It's sharded.

00:29:51.630 --> 00:29:54.630
And so the full communication
remains the same.

00:29:54.630 --> 00:29:57.200
So ZeRO stage 2 has
some more overhead

00:29:57.200 --> 00:29:59.540
because we have to
synchronize layer by layer

00:29:59.540 --> 00:30:01.760
and make sure that the
gradients are properly

00:30:01.760 --> 00:30:03.530
sent to the right workers.

00:30:03.530 --> 00:30:05.480
But the overhead
is pretty minimal.

00:30:05.480 --> 00:30:08.450
It's still very simple,
fairly straightforward.

00:30:08.450 --> 00:30:11.600
Now the last one of
these ZeRO stage 3

00:30:11.600 --> 00:30:14.540
is more complicated
for sure, but it

00:30:14.540 --> 00:30:17.630
allows you the greatest win of
all, which is now essentially

00:30:17.630 --> 00:30:21.390
everything is divided by the
number of GPUs that you have.

00:30:21.390 --> 00:30:24.330
And you can get the
maximum savings possible.

00:30:24.330 --> 00:30:27.110
And if you've heard
of FSDP, you've

00:30:27.110 --> 00:30:31.310
probably used that in some
aspect of your life in the past.

00:30:31.310 --> 00:30:33.510
FSDP is exactly ZeRO stage 3.

00:30:33.510 --> 00:30:38.160
So now you'll kind of hopefully
today know how FSDP works.

00:30:38.160 --> 00:30:40.710
So the same idea applies.

00:30:40.710 --> 00:30:43.238
We're going to shard everything,
including the parameters.

00:30:43.238 --> 00:30:45.530
We're going to do the same
thing as ZeRO stage 2, which

00:30:45.530 --> 00:30:48.290
is we're going to incrementally
communicate and compute things

00:30:48.290 --> 00:30:51.770
so that we don't keep these
big vectors of gradients lying

00:30:51.770 --> 00:30:52.733
around.

00:30:52.733 --> 00:30:54.650
And we're going to send
and request parameters

00:30:54.650 --> 00:30:58.010
on demand while we're stepping
through the compute graph, both

00:30:58.010 --> 00:30:59.990
for the forward and
backward passes.

00:30:59.990 --> 00:31:03.320
As we go through, we're going
to send things around on demand.

00:31:03.320 --> 00:31:06.560
And of course, the key is to
do this with as low overhead as

00:31:06.560 --> 00:31:07.910
possible.

00:31:07.910 --> 00:31:10.600
I think the thing that's
really surprising about FSDP

00:31:10.600 --> 00:31:13.030
is not that this is
possible, but that this

00:31:13.030 --> 00:31:15.820
is possible with
relatively low overhead.

00:31:15.820 --> 00:31:21.130
You'll see why it's low
overhead in the next slide.

00:31:21.130 --> 00:31:25.827
I admit that this is maybe
not the most friendly graphic

00:31:25.827 --> 00:31:27.410
to start with, but
this is, I promise,

00:31:27.410 --> 00:31:30.190
the baby version of FSDP.

00:31:30.190 --> 00:31:32.920
The next slide is a
little bit more involved.

00:31:32.920 --> 00:31:35.180
But conceptually, this
actually explains everything.

00:31:35.180 --> 00:31:39.170
So what we're doing is we're
going to have model weights,

00:31:39.170 --> 00:31:41.770
and we're going to be
all gathering the model

00:31:41.770 --> 00:31:42.530
weights as we go.

00:31:42.530 --> 00:31:46.270
So for each layer, no
single GPU is going

00:31:46.270 --> 00:31:47.510
to have all the parameters.

00:31:47.510 --> 00:31:50.360
So I can't do the normal
thing of saying oh GPU 0,

00:31:50.360 --> 00:31:51.860
go ahead and run
the forward pass.

00:31:51.860 --> 00:31:52.880
That's not possible.

00:31:52.880 --> 00:31:57.865
So GPU 0, let's say, only
owns the bottom most layer.

00:31:57.865 --> 00:31:59.740
So it does that computation
and then it stops

00:31:59.740 --> 00:32:02.660
and it says-- it requests
the parameters from all

00:32:02.660 --> 00:32:03.410
the other workers.

00:32:03.410 --> 00:32:05.950
So it stops and it does all
gather, which is right here.

00:32:05.950 --> 00:32:07.730
You see there's the
all gather step.

00:32:07.730 --> 00:32:09.200
It gathers all the parameters.

00:32:09.200 --> 00:32:13.580
Now it has the parameters
that it needs to do a forward.

00:32:13.580 --> 00:32:16.540
So it can step forward
and compute the layer

00:32:16.540 --> 00:32:17.960
that it didn't have before.

00:32:17.960 --> 00:32:19.502
And then now it can
free the weights.

00:32:19.502 --> 00:32:21.002
It doesn't need the
weights anymore.

00:32:21.002 --> 00:32:21.770
Get rid of it.

00:32:21.770 --> 00:32:23.720
Now it can all gather
the next layer,

00:32:23.720 --> 00:32:25.600
I can do another
forward, free the weights

00:32:25.600 --> 00:32:26.840
and I can repeat this right.

00:32:26.840 --> 00:32:28.310
The activations
have to be stored.

00:32:28.310 --> 00:32:30.890
So the activation
memory here is growing.

00:32:30.890 --> 00:32:33.007
So that's going to be
an eventual problem.

00:32:33.007 --> 00:32:34.840
But if we ignore
activations for the moment,

00:32:34.840 --> 00:32:36.860
this is great because
I load a layer,

00:32:36.860 --> 00:32:38.390
I do a forward, I free it.

00:32:38.390 --> 00:32:40.370
The memory overhead
is very low here.

00:32:40.370 --> 00:32:43.510
Once I get to the end, now
I can do the same thing

00:32:43.510 --> 00:32:44.500
with the backward pass.

00:32:44.500 --> 00:32:46.300
I can call backwards,
and every time I

00:32:46.300 --> 00:32:49.150
move backwards through
the neural network,

00:32:49.150 --> 00:32:52.030
I all gather for the
parameters that I need.

00:32:52.030 --> 00:32:55.990
I can do a reduce scatter to
update after the gradients that

00:32:55.990 --> 00:32:58.220
have been computed, and
now I can free the weights.

00:32:58.220 --> 00:33:00.340
So I can free both the
gradients that I don't need

00:33:00.340 --> 00:33:01.370
and the parameters.

00:33:01.370 --> 00:33:04.910
And at the very end, I've
got a fully updated model.

00:33:04.910 --> 00:33:06.720
And so we've got three
different operations

00:33:06.720 --> 00:33:07.930
that we've got to
worry about here.

00:33:07.930 --> 00:33:10.180
We've got to all gather,
we've got another all gather,

00:33:10.180 --> 00:33:11.980
and then we've got
another reduce scatter,

00:33:11.980 --> 00:33:14.670
basically to update
the model after we

00:33:14.670 --> 00:33:17.490
take the gradient update step.

00:33:17.490 --> 00:33:23.890
So conceptually, this is just a
single step beyond ZeRO stage 2,

00:33:23.890 --> 00:33:27.550
but you do kind see that
there is more overhead.

00:33:27.550 --> 00:33:30.220
So the total communication
cost is now higher.

00:33:30.220 --> 00:33:32.667
We were kind of-- before,
we had two times the number

00:33:32.667 --> 00:33:33.250
of parameters.

00:33:33.250 --> 00:33:35.110
Everything was kind
of free in some sense.

00:33:35.110 --> 00:33:36.220
Now it's not right.

00:33:36.220 --> 00:33:38.370
There's a total of three
times number of parameter

00:33:38.370 --> 00:33:40.410
communication cost
and there's going

00:33:40.410 --> 00:33:44.100
to be cost associated with
waiting for these communication

00:33:44.100 --> 00:33:44.900
things to finish.

00:33:47.580 --> 00:33:50.400
But I think the really
cool thing about FSDP

00:33:50.400 --> 00:33:54.180
is it's actually
surprisingly low overhead.

00:33:54.180 --> 00:33:56.280
You might imagine
that because we're

00:33:56.280 --> 00:33:59.640
doing this crazy thing of asking
for and sending parameters

00:33:59.640 --> 00:34:03.510
back and forth all the time,
that things will be really slow.

00:34:03.510 --> 00:34:05.230
We have to be
communicating all the time.

00:34:05.230 --> 00:34:09.270
But you can do this core idea
of overlapping communication

00:34:09.270 --> 00:34:10.239
and computation.

00:34:10.239 --> 00:34:14.340
So you want your
GPU to be working,

00:34:14.340 --> 00:34:16.840
while also the communication
is happening in the background,

00:34:16.840 --> 00:34:19.320
almost like prefetching,
so that by the time

00:34:19.320 --> 00:34:21.010
you need some piece
of information,

00:34:21.010 --> 00:34:23.310
it's already loaded up, it's
already been communicated

00:34:23.310 --> 00:34:25.260
to you, and you're good to go.

00:34:25.260 --> 00:34:28.350
And so I'll talk through this
example at the bottom here.

00:34:28.350 --> 00:34:31.770
But this is kind of the
key to making FSDP actually

00:34:31.770 --> 00:34:33.510
somewhat efficient.

00:34:33.510 --> 00:34:35.969
So let's imagine we have a
computation graph that looks

00:34:35.969 --> 00:34:37.000
something like this--

00:34:37.000 --> 00:34:40.780
W1 W0 plus W2 W0 times x.

00:34:40.780 --> 00:34:41.969
Some input let's say is y.

00:34:41.969 --> 00:34:44.770
So some very simple
computation graph like this.

00:34:44.770 --> 00:34:47.830
And then you might run FSDP.

00:34:47.830 --> 00:34:50.670
And you will get actually
computation and communication

00:34:50.670 --> 00:34:54.810
that looks like this block
diagram at the very end here.

00:34:54.810 --> 00:34:59.520
So the CPU it's nice that we
did the nsight systems example

00:34:59.520 --> 00:35:02.230
last week because hopefully
this diagram will now be clear.

00:35:02.230 --> 00:35:06.640
The CPU is going to basically
dispatch a bunch of commands,

00:35:06.640 --> 00:35:10.510
asking the communication
part of the GPU

00:35:10.510 --> 00:35:13.490
to basically go and
fetch some parameters.

00:35:13.490 --> 00:35:15.710
It's going to dispatch
things to the GPU to say,

00:35:15.710 --> 00:35:17.470
OK, all right, do
some matrix multiplies

00:35:17.470 --> 00:35:21.190
and it's going to run far
ahead in some sense of the GPU.

00:35:21.190 --> 00:35:23.680
We've seen this when we
were looking at the profiler

00:35:23.680 --> 00:35:25.090
last week.

00:35:25.090 --> 00:35:27.940
Now let's look at the
sequence of both communication

00:35:27.940 --> 00:35:31.330
and computation that
happens on device now.

00:35:31.330 --> 00:35:34.073
Remember that I need to
gather things on demand.

00:35:34.073 --> 00:35:35.740
So at the very
beginning, I have to make

00:35:35.740 --> 00:35:40.370
sure that everyone has the
weights for layer 0, or W0 here.

00:35:40.370 --> 00:35:42.760
So I do all gather
0, and I'm going

00:35:42.760 --> 00:35:44.000
to wait for that to complete.

00:35:44.000 --> 00:35:48.110
And once that's completed, I
can do a forward step on W0.

00:35:48.110 --> 00:35:51.400
I can compute x
times W0, let's say.

00:35:51.400 --> 00:35:55.270
At this point, all gather
1 starts at the same time

00:35:55.270 --> 00:35:56.900
that all gather 0 ends.

00:35:56.900 --> 00:35:59.180
So as I'm doing this
matrix multiply,

00:35:59.180 --> 00:36:01.700
I'm basically already starting
to load the next parameters

00:36:01.700 --> 00:36:02.400
that I need.

00:36:02.400 --> 00:36:04.500
Of course, my
communication is slower.

00:36:04.500 --> 00:36:07.940
And so there is some gap,
but I end much quicker

00:36:07.940 --> 00:36:09.210
than the initial load.

00:36:09.210 --> 00:36:10.903
So now forward 1 can happen.

00:36:10.903 --> 00:36:12.320
And in the background,
once again,

00:36:12.320 --> 00:36:15.390
I've started to load
parameter number 2.

00:36:15.390 --> 00:36:17.270
And this yellow
slice here, I'm now

00:36:17.270 --> 00:36:20.280
freeing the parameters
associated with forward 1.

00:36:20.280 --> 00:36:21.860
And then now the
other thing here

00:36:21.860 --> 00:36:24.613
is I'm repeating computation
W0 is used twice.

00:36:24.613 --> 00:36:26.280
And so I don't need
to communicate this.

00:36:26.280 --> 00:36:28.350
Again this happens very quickly.

00:36:28.350 --> 00:36:30.900
And I can do this very quickly.

00:36:30.900 --> 00:36:35.700
I have forward 2 now already
loaded before I needed it.

00:36:35.700 --> 00:36:37.072
And so there's no bubble here.

00:36:37.072 --> 00:36:38.280
And then I can free number 2.

00:36:38.280 --> 00:36:39.840
That's the entirety
of the forward pass.

00:36:39.840 --> 00:36:42.030
And you see that the gaps
are relatively small here.

00:36:42.030 --> 00:36:44.000
And we were able to
do a lot of loads

00:36:44.000 --> 00:36:46.380
before the compute
needed to happen.

00:36:46.380 --> 00:36:50.180
And so by doing this very clever
thing of queuing the requests

00:36:50.180 --> 00:36:53.400
for waits before you
actually need them,

00:36:53.400 --> 00:36:55.820
you can avoid a lot of
the overhead associated

00:36:55.820 --> 00:36:57.530
with communication.

00:36:57.530 --> 00:37:02.220
And then now at this
point of forward 2,

00:37:02.220 --> 00:37:03.610
I'm done with the forward pass.

00:37:03.610 --> 00:37:05.080
I can free weight number 2.

00:37:05.080 --> 00:37:06.550
And I start on
the backward pass.

00:37:06.550 --> 00:37:09.150
And you see that all gather
2 for the backward pass

00:37:09.150 --> 00:37:10.150
is already done.

00:37:10.150 --> 00:37:12.750
And so I can start on
backward 2, backward 0.

00:37:12.750 --> 00:37:13.960
Weight 0 is already stored.

00:37:13.960 --> 00:37:14.770
So that's done.

00:37:14.770 --> 00:37:17.460
And then the high overhead here
happens in the backward pass

00:37:17.460 --> 00:37:20.070
because I need to do reduce
scatters and then all gathers

00:37:20.070 --> 00:37:21.038
and so on and so forth.

00:37:21.038 --> 00:37:23.080
Hopefully, you see this
picture and you say, wow,

00:37:23.080 --> 00:37:25.470
it's kind of surprising that
even though we're doing this

00:37:25.470 --> 00:37:28.320
crazy sharding-- if you
go back to this picture,

00:37:28.320 --> 00:37:32.070
we fully sharded the parameters
gradients and optimizer states--

00:37:32.070 --> 00:37:35.400
but the total bandwidth
that we need is only 3 times

00:37:35.400 --> 00:37:36.242
rather than 2 times.

00:37:36.242 --> 00:37:37.450
So that doesn't seem too bad.

00:37:37.450 --> 00:37:41.340
And the actual bubbles that
we see are not horrendous.

00:37:41.340 --> 00:37:44.850
The communication is almost
being fully being utilized

00:37:44.850 --> 00:37:47.620
and the computation isn't
stalling for very long.

00:37:47.620 --> 00:37:50.340
So we're actually making pretty
efficient use of the resources

00:37:50.340 --> 00:37:53.700
that we do have, which is cool.

00:37:53.700 --> 00:37:55.140
OK, yes.

00:37:55.140 --> 00:37:57.670
Where do I get [INAUDIBLE].

00:37:57.670 --> 00:38:00.660
It's like to my understanding,
the GPU and memory

00:38:00.660 --> 00:38:04.570
is full, so where does the
weights get prefetched?

00:38:04.570 --> 00:38:06.053
Yeah, so you need
a buffer in which

00:38:06.053 --> 00:38:07.220
you can store these weights.

00:38:07.220 --> 00:38:10.700
And so this picture
is not quite right.

00:38:10.700 --> 00:38:12.630
You will have some
overhead that you

00:38:12.630 --> 00:38:14.380
need associated with
reading these weights

00:38:14.380 --> 00:38:15.350
for the current layer.

00:38:15.350 --> 00:38:17.200
And also, the other big
elephant in the room

00:38:17.200 --> 00:38:19.160
is I haven't talked at
all about activation.

00:38:19.160 --> 00:38:20.450
That's going to be
like a big chunk,

00:38:20.450 --> 00:38:22.242
because you've got a
big set of activations

00:38:22.242 --> 00:38:25.250
for a full model that are of
living here in some sense.

00:38:25.250 --> 00:38:26.205
Yeah, cool.

00:38:28.820 --> 00:38:32.050
So this is kind of
distributed data parallel.

00:38:32.050 --> 00:38:36.220
0 is, in some ways, the way
that people do distributed data

00:38:36.220 --> 00:38:37.900
parallel efficiently.

00:38:37.900 --> 00:38:39.740
And so there's different stages.

00:38:39.740 --> 00:38:43.910
And stage 1 is it's
basically free.

00:38:43.910 --> 00:38:46.960
It's doing the same
communication pattern

00:38:46.960 --> 00:38:50.320
as naive data
parallel, but you get

00:38:50.320 --> 00:38:51.650
to shard your optimizer state.

00:38:51.650 --> 00:38:52.192
That's great.

00:38:52.192 --> 00:38:53.570
You might as well always do it.

00:38:53.570 --> 00:38:56.510
ZeRO stage 2 is twice
the number of parameters.

00:38:56.510 --> 00:38:58.710
So the total bandwidth
consumption is the same,

00:38:58.710 --> 00:39:00.980
but there is additional
overhead in having

00:39:00.980 --> 00:39:03.890
to do this incremental
freeing of the gradients

00:39:03.890 --> 00:39:05.600
as you go backwards.

00:39:05.600 --> 00:39:07.320
ZeRO stage 3 is more involved.

00:39:07.320 --> 00:39:09.990
You do three times the number
of program communication cost,

00:39:09.990 --> 00:39:11.450
but it's not so bad.

00:39:11.450 --> 00:39:14.850
We did have some overhead in
the diagram that we saw before.

00:39:14.850 --> 00:39:17.900
But if you really cleverly mask
your communication patterns,

00:39:17.900 --> 00:39:19.590
it's actually pretty good.

00:39:19.590 --> 00:39:21.470
And so people use
data parallel even

00:39:21.470 --> 00:39:26.750
for fairly slow links in
your networking pattern.

00:39:26.750 --> 00:39:29.130
And this is also
conceptually very simple.

00:39:29.130 --> 00:39:32.390
One of the advantages
here is especially

00:39:32.390 --> 00:39:34.920
data parallel doesn't care too
much about the architecture.

00:39:34.920 --> 00:39:37.430
I didn't talk at all about
how we actually implement

00:39:37.430 --> 00:39:38.880
a transformer in any of this.

00:39:38.880 --> 00:39:40.140
It's all very abstracted.

00:39:40.140 --> 00:39:42.950
And so this is one of the
reasons why, for example, FSDP

00:39:42.950 --> 00:39:45.350
is so popular, it's
very easy to write

00:39:45.350 --> 00:39:49.100
a wrapper that parallelizes
arbitrary neural networks

00:39:49.100 --> 00:39:52.880
without having deep knowledge
or deep introspection of what

00:39:52.880 --> 00:39:55.740
the architecture
is actually doing.

00:39:55.740 --> 00:39:57.630
And so here's some examples.

00:39:57.630 --> 00:40:00.090
I worked out some examples
because I'm always running out

00:40:00.090 --> 00:40:02.070
of memory on my GPUs.

00:40:02.070 --> 00:40:04.830
And you can see what's the
maximum size of the model

00:40:04.830 --> 00:40:10.140
that I can fit on an eight
times A100 80 gig node.

00:40:10.140 --> 00:40:12.370
And so for baseline, you
might end up with oh,

00:40:12.370 --> 00:40:15.990
I can fit barely 6
billion parameter model.

00:40:15.990 --> 00:40:18.310
Whereas I think if
I use ZeRO stage 3,

00:40:18.310 --> 00:40:21.010
I'm able to fit something like
a 50 billion parameter model.

00:40:21.010 --> 00:40:24.330
There's big savings
in my ability

00:40:24.330 --> 00:40:27.780
to fit larger and larger models
by doing things like FSDP

00:40:27.780 --> 00:40:32.450
to cleverly save on memory.

00:40:32.450 --> 00:40:33.700
Oh, sorry, there's a question.

00:40:33.700 --> 00:40:35.140
Yes.

00:40:35.140 --> 00:40:37.950
I guess I'm a little unclear as
to where the difference then,

00:40:37.950 --> 00:40:39.510
once you shard the
parameters, what's

00:40:39.510 --> 00:40:41.310
the difference between
that [INAUDIBLE]?

00:40:41.310 --> 00:40:45.300
Yeah, so model parallelism
is really fundamentally

00:40:45.300 --> 00:40:48.030
about making sure that
the parameters just

00:40:48.030 --> 00:40:51.057
like live in separate--

00:40:51.057 --> 00:40:52.140
let me see if I can find--

00:40:52.140 --> 00:40:53.680
[INAUDIBLE]

00:40:54.220 --> 00:40:55.280
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:40:55.280 --> 00:40:58.910
So in some ways, it's true that
we have sharded the parameters.

00:40:58.910 --> 00:41:01.630
So you could call this
a kind of parallelism.

00:41:01.630 --> 00:41:03.580
But the whole point
of model parallelism

00:41:03.580 --> 00:41:06.070
is to make sure that the
parameters just live entirely

00:41:06.070 --> 00:41:06.800
in one machine.

00:41:06.800 --> 00:41:09.410
We're not going to try to ship
them across in various ways.

00:41:09.410 --> 00:41:12.305
Only the activations are
going to get shipped across.

00:41:12.305 --> 00:41:14.680
And so you'll see very different
discussions in the model

00:41:14.680 --> 00:41:15.730
parallelism section.

00:41:15.730 --> 00:41:18.340
The focus there will be on
communicating activations rather

00:41:18.340 --> 00:41:20.353
than communicating parameters.

00:41:20.353 --> 00:41:21.770
And that will be
a big difference.

00:41:21.770 --> 00:41:24.010
Yes.

00:41:24.010 --> 00:41:27.940
If the parameters
[INAUDIBLE], Why are you

00:41:27.940 --> 00:41:29.662
performing at all [INAUDIBLE]?

00:41:32.770 --> 00:41:36.680
So you're asking
about this step,

00:41:36.680 --> 00:41:39.100
why are we doing all
gather to gather weights

00:41:39.100 --> 00:41:41.870
onto all the machines when
they're only on one machine.

00:41:41.870 --> 00:41:42.580
Is that right?

00:41:42.580 --> 00:41:45.508
Yeah, so we need
to basically put--

00:41:45.508 --> 00:41:47.050
we need to take the
weights that live

00:41:47.050 --> 00:41:48.440
on one machine and scatter--

00:41:48.440 --> 00:41:50.240
or is it gather or scatter?

00:41:50.240 --> 00:41:52.390
Sorry, I want to make
sure I get this right.

00:41:55.130 --> 00:41:59.670
The terminology is a
little bit sketchy for me.

00:41:59.670 --> 00:42:02.120
So I want to make sure I get--

00:42:02.120 --> 00:42:04.160
sorry.

00:42:04.160 --> 00:42:04.860
Yeah.

00:42:04.860 --> 00:42:07.670
So what we want to do
is the same as this.

00:42:07.670 --> 00:42:11.480
So each machine is going
to have some parameter

00:42:11.480 --> 00:42:15.080
that I want to gather
across all of the machines

00:42:15.080 --> 00:42:19.310
in order to make sure that
each layer is properly

00:42:19.310 --> 00:42:22.473
replicated across all the GPUs.

00:42:22.473 --> 00:42:24.390
Is that the right question
that you're asking?

00:42:24.390 --> 00:42:26.840
Or are you saying
is there a simpler

00:42:26.840 --> 00:42:29.060
primitive that we
could have invoked?

00:42:29.060 --> 00:42:32.330
Are you saying broadcast is
