00:42:38.370 --> 00:42:39.410
but I'm not 100% sure.

00:42:39.410 --> 00:42:41.120
I agree with you
that broadcast should

00:42:41.120 --> 00:42:44.750
be able to do the same thing if
the parameters live on only one

00:42:44.750 --> 00:42:45.250
machine.

00:42:47.930 --> 00:42:49.600
OK, cool.

00:42:49.600 --> 00:42:51.650
Alrighty.

00:42:51.650 --> 00:42:56.290
Let me make sure we're--

00:42:56.290 --> 00:42:58.310
got it.

00:42:58.310 --> 00:43:02.030
So there is a key
resource in data parallel.

00:43:02.030 --> 00:43:04.270
And this is actually
an important idea

00:43:04.270 --> 00:43:06.340
that I want you to remember.

00:43:06.340 --> 00:43:08.890
With data parallel,
batch size is actually

00:43:08.890 --> 00:43:11.350
a really critical
resource in the sense

00:43:11.350 --> 00:43:14.440
that you can't parallelize
greater than your number--

00:43:14.440 --> 00:43:17.320
sorry-- than your batch size
because you can have at most one

00:43:17.320 --> 00:43:19.430
example on each machine.

00:43:19.430 --> 00:43:22.510
You can't go to fractional
examples per machine.

00:43:22.510 --> 00:43:25.390
And so this means
that if there's

00:43:25.390 --> 00:43:28.270
limits to your batch
size, you stop being

00:43:28.270 --> 00:43:30.620
able to use data parallel.

00:43:30.620 --> 00:43:32.900
And there's diminishing
returns to batch sizes.

00:43:32.900 --> 00:43:35.110
So in your assignment
one, you may have played

00:43:35.110 --> 00:43:36.590
with varying batch sizes.

00:43:36.590 --> 00:43:39.250
But you kind of know that as
you crank up the batch size

00:43:39.250 --> 00:43:43.690
past a certain point, you start
to see fairly rapid diminishing

00:43:43.690 --> 00:43:47.770
returns to your
optimization rates.

00:43:47.770 --> 00:43:49.770
And there's lots of
papers written on this.

00:43:49.770 --> 00:43:52.130
OpenAI has a really
nice one on something

00:43:52.130 --> 00:43:54.980
called critical batch
sizes, where they basically

00:43:54.980 --> 00:43:56.900
argue that past a
certain point, you

00:43:56.900 --> 00:44:00.020
have very rapid diminishing
returns in how much

00:44:00.020 --> 00:44:03.180
each example is contributing
to your ability to optimize.

00:44:03.180 --> 00:44:05.790
Basically, the intuition is
that below a certain point,

00:44:05.790 --> 00:44:07.820
you have a lot of gradient
noise and reducing

00:44:07.820 --> 00:44:09.000
that is very valuable.

00:44:09.000 --> 00:44:10.760
But at a certain
point, you're really

00:44:10.760 --> 00:44:12.650
fundamentally
limited by the number

00:44:12.650 --> 00:44:16.160
of gradient steps you're taking
rather than variance reduction.

00:44:16.160 --> 00:44:18.770
And so that basically
means data parallel alone

00:44:18.770 --> 00:44:21.720
isn't going to get you to
arbitrarily large parallelism.

00:44:21.720 --> 00:44:25.250
And the batch size thing is
a really important resource.

00:44:25.250 --> 00:44:27.630
Essentially, you have a
fixed maximum batch size,

00:44:27.630 --> 00:44:30.230
and you can spend it
in different ways.

00:44:30.230 --> 00:44:32.330
And I'll talk about that
later because other kinds

00:44:32.330 --> 00:44:36.360
of parallelism also benefit
from having bigger batches.

00:44:36.360 --> 00:44:39.060
And so you use your batch
size in certain parts.

00:44:39.060 --> 00:44:43.370
And issues are going to
remain with data parallel.

00:44:43.370 --> 00:44:46.250
ZeRO stages 1 and 2 don't
let you scale memory.

00:44:46.250 --> 00:44:49.810
ZeRO stage 3 is nice in
principle, but it can be slow.

00:44:49.810 --> 00:44:52.428
And maybe more importantly--
and this relates

00:44:52.428 --> 00:44:53.970
to the earlier
question-- it does not

00:44:53.970 --> 00:44:56.280
reduce activation memory.

00:44:56.280 --> 00:44:59.690
Ideally, you want to cut up
my model entirely and make

00:44:59.690 --> 00:45:01.440
them live totally
separately, because then

00:45:01.440 --> 00:45:05.110
the activation memory
would also be reduced.

00:45:05.110 --> 00:45:07.560
And so now I want
better ways to split up

00:45:07.560 --> 00:45:14.100
the model so I can fit these
really big models in these GPUs.

00:45:14.100 --> 00:45:18.240
And so that's going to bring
us to model parallelism.

00:45:18.240 --> 00:45:22.450
We want to scale up in memory
without changing the batch size.

00:45:22.450 --> 00:45:24.510
And we want an
alternative axis where

00:45:24.510 --> 00:45:27.030
we don't need to spend or
basically have big batch

00:45:27.030 --> 00:45:29.850
sizes in order to parallelize.

00:45:29.850 --> 00:45:31.620
And so what we're
going to do is it's

00:45:31.620 --> 00:45:34.180
going to split up the
parameters across GPUs.

00:45:34.180 --> 00:45:36.640
And in some ways,
that's like ZeRO 3.

00:45:36.640 --> 00:45:38.890
But we're not going to
communicate parameters anymore.

00:45:38.890 --> 00:45:40.590
We're going to pass
activations around.

00:45:40.590 --> 00:45:42.150
And that's going
to be different.

00:45:42.150 --> 00:45:43.650
And sometimes
activations are going

00:45:43.650 --> 00:45:45.430
to be much smaller
than parameters.

00:45:45.430 --> 00:45:47.710
And that'll be very good for us.

00:45:47.710 --> 00:45:50.270
So we'll cover two different
types of parallelism.

00:45:50.270 --> 00:45:52.270
I'm going to talk about
pipeline parallel, which

00:45:52.270 --> 00:45:54.820
is conceptually
simpler, but much more

00:45:54.820 --> 00:45:57.820
horrible implementation wise,
and tensor parallel, which

00:45:57.820 --> 00:46:02.530
is conceptually maybe less
obvious, but honestly much nicer

00:46:02.530 --> 00:46:04.900
to implement and
more commonly used.

00:46:04.900 --> 00:46:07.660
And they're going to correspond
to two different ways of cutting

00:46:07.660 --> 00:46:09.190
up the model.

00:46:09.190 --> 00:46:13.360
So I think pipeline parallel
is maybe the most obvious way

00:46:13.360 --> 00:46:14.670
to cut up a neural network.

00:46:14.670 --> 00:46:18.160
You know that a deep neural
network comes in layers.

00:46:18.160 --> 00:46:21.220
So if I have layers, a very
natural place to cut a network

00:46:21.220 --> 00:46:23.600
is to cut it up at
the layer boundaries.

00:46:23.600 --> 00:46:25.990
And so each GPU
is going to handle

00:46:25.990 --> 00:46:27.850
some subset of the
layers, and I'm

00:46:27.850 --> 00:46:29.560
going to pass
activations around.

00:46:29.560 --> 00:46:31.820
In this case, each
layer belongs to a GPU.

00:46:31.820 --> 00:46:34.450
And GPUs are going
to pass activations

00:46:34.450 --> 00:46:35.548
from one to the other.

00:46:35.548 --> 00:46:37.090
And in the backwards
case, it's going

00:46:37.090 --> 00:46:42.790
to pass the backwards gradients
backwards from GPU 3 to 0.

00:46:42.790 --> 00:46:45.300
So that's cool.

00:46:45.300 --> 00:46:46.430
That's great.

00:46:46.430 --> 00:46:48.300
What's wrong with this picture?

00:46:48.300 --> 00:46:51.260
Well, I think you should
see that most of your GPUs

00:46:51.260 --> 00:46:52.890
are idle most of the time.

00:46:52.890 --> 00:46:55.730
This is actually quite
terrible utilization.

00:46:55.730 --> 00:46:58.400
And so if I do this
naive kind of parallelism

00:46:58.400 --> 00:47:01.070
that I described
before-- so if I have,

00:47:01.070 --> 00:47:03.440
each layer having a
forward and let's say

00:47:03.440 --> 00:47:05.150
I have a single
example, that's going

00:47:05.150 --> 00:47:07.080
to result in a diagram
that looks like this.

00:47:07.080 --> 00:47:09.800
So different rows
in this picture

00:47:09.800 --> 00:47:13.920
are different layers
and also different GPUs.

00:47:13.920 --> 00:47:15.950
And the x-axis
here is time where

00:47:15.950 --> 00:47:17.690
I'm going from left to right.

00:47:17.690 --> 00:47:18.720
So what do you see?

00:47:18.720 --> 00:47:21.935
Well, I first compute my first
layer at the very left here.

00:47:21.935 --> 00:47:24.060
And then the activations
get past the second layer.

00:47:24.060 --> 00:47:26.370
GPU 2 wakes up and it's like,
all right, it's my turn.

00:47:26.370 --> 00:47:29.850
It does its job, passes
to GPU 3 and then GPU 4.

00:47:29.850 --> 00:47:33.030
And now the backwards passes can
begin, and so on and so forth.

00:47:33.030 --> 00:47:35.880
And you see this gigantic
What people call bubble.

00:47:35.880 --> 00:47:39.440
This is a big overhead where
you're doing absolutely nothing.

00:47:39.440 --> 00:47:43.050
And you see that the GPUs are
active 1 over n of the time.

00:47:43.050 --> 00:47:45.840
So in some sense, this is the
worst possible parallelism

00:47:45.840 --> 00:47:50.280
of I've added 4 GPUs, but I get
the throughput of a single GPU.

00:47:50.280 --> 00:47:55.230
And so one thing
you can do is you

00:47:55.230 --> 00:47:58.092
can be a little bit more
clever about what you do,

00:47:58.092 --> 00:48:00.300
and you say, all right, I'm
going to have a pipeline.

00:48:00.300 --> 00:48:02.290
I'm not just going to
cut things up in layers.

00:48:02.290 --> 00:48:04.620
I'm going to have a
sequence of things that need

00:48:04.620 --> 00:48:06.280
to be processed by each GPU.

00:48:06.280 --> 00:48:09.330
So now let's say I
have a microbatch.

00:48:09.330 --> 00:48:13.260
So each machine is going
to handle four examples.

00:48:13.260 --> 00:48:17.530
And what I'm going to do is I
can finish my first example,

00:48:17.530 --> 00:48:20.550
my first data point,
and I can send off

00:48:20.550 --> 00:48:24.520
the activations for that to my
second GPU as soon as I finish.

00:48:24.520 --> 00:48:27.790
And then I can then get started
working on my second data point.

00:48:27.790 --> 00:48:32.020
And so now I've overlapped
communication and computation.

00:48:32.020 --> 00:48:35.460
The second GPU can start working
while the first GPU continues

00:48:35.460 --> 00:48:36.070
to work.

00:48:36.070 --> 00:48:38.460
And now the size of the
bubble can potentially

00:48:38.460 --> 00:48:41.230
be reduced by having
bigger batch sizes.

00:48:41.230 --> 00:48:44.950
And you can hopefully see why I
said before that batch sizes are

00:48:44.950 --> 00:48:45.620
a resource.

00:48:45.620 --> 00:48:48.500
If you have a finite batch size,
and you have pipeline parallel,

00:48:48.500 --> 00:48:51.220
you can use that same batch size
to make your pipeline bubble

00:48:51.220 --> 00:48:53.410
size smaller, for
example, or you could

00:48:53.410 --> 00:48:55.055
use it to do data parallel.

00:48:55.055 --> 00:48:56.680
So there's many
different ways that you

00:48:56.680 --> 00:48:59.500
can take your single batch
size and then split it up

00:48:59.500 --> 00:49:01.390
in two different ways.

00:49:01.390 --> 00:49:06.410
So now your micro batch size
can control the bubble time.

00:49:06.410 --> 00:49:09.070
And in fact, the
amount of the ratio

00:49:09.070 --> 00:49:11.890
of your overhead to the
useful compute that you have

00:49:11.890 --> 00:49:15.280
is the number of stages
minus 1 over the number

00:49:15.280 --> 00:49:16.280
of micro batches.

00:49:16.280 --> 00:49:18.530
So if you have big
big batch sizes,

00:49:18.530 --> 00:49:20.750
pipeline parallel could
potentially be efficient.

00:49:20.750 --> 00:49:24.230
But as we said before,
batch sizes are finite.

00:49:24.230 --> 00:49:28.030
We can't just crank that up to
whatever value that we want.

00:49:28.030 --> 00:49:33.190
So in general, pipelines
seem really horrible.

00:49:33.190 --> 00:49:34.190
Why do we do it?

00:49:34.190 --> 00:49:39.030
Why do we incur this cost of a
bubble in order to parallelize?

00:49:39.030 --> 00:49:40.370
Well, there's a couple reasons.

00:49:40.370 --> 00:49:44.340
Pipelines help save memory
compared to data parallel.

00:49:44.340 --> 00:49:47.040
I mean, ZeRO 3 will also
shard the parameters,

00:49:47.040 --> 00:49:50.510
but this also shards the
activations, which is nice.

00:49:50.510 --> 00:49:53.220
Pipelines can also have good
communication properties.

00:49:53.220 --> 00:49:55.140
It only depends on activations.

00:49:55.140 --> 00:49:56.400
It's also point to point.

00:49:56.400 --> 00:49:58.700
So it's possible that
depending on your topology

00:49:58.700 --> 00:50:01.130
and depending on what you
have, pipelines might actually

00:50:01.130 --> 00:50:05.480
be very favorable for the
slower parts of your network.

00:50:05.480 --> 00:50:07.970
And so pipeline
parallel is often

00:50:07.970 --> 00:50:10.440
going to be used on your
slower network links.

00:50:10.440 --> 00:50:15.680
So internode or even sometimes
across different racks

00:50:15.680 --> 00:50:17.160
or across different
data centers,

00:50:17.160 --> 00:50:18.290
you might do--
actually, not data

00:50:18.290 --> 00:50:19.740
centers-- across
different racks,

00:50:19.740 --> 00:50:21.180
you might do pipeline parallel.

00:50:21.180 --> 00:50:24.980
One of the examples of a
thing that I was recently

00:50:24.980 --> 00:50:28.050
told by some Google folks is,
they were saying, actually,

00:50:28.050 --> 00:50:29.900
one of the big
advantages of TPUs

00:50:29.900 --> 00:50:32.090
is that we don't have to
do pipeline parallel very

00:50:32.090 --> 00:50:35.370
much because all of our
connections are much bigger.

00:50:35.370 --> 00:50:37.080
They have this
big toroidal mesh.

00:50:37.080 --> 00:50:39.990
They don't have this limit at
256 GPUs, where they're suddenly

00:50:39.990 --> 00:50:42.420
going towards a slower network
link, where you might want

00:50:42.420 --> 00:50:43.830
to switch to pipeline parallel.

00:50:43.830 --> 00:50:45.600
So that's a real
world kind of example

00:50:45.600 --> 00:50:48.690
of when you would start to
think about pipeline parallel.

00:50:48.690 --> 00:50:51.220
And so this is an example
from an NVIDIA paper.

00:50:51.220 --> 00:50:54.660
I'll talk about this paper
in much greater detail later.

00:50:54.660 --> 00:50:56.400
They've done some
really nice work

00:50:56.400 --> 00:50:58.080
showing performance
characteristics

00:50:58.080 --> 00:50:59.530
of different kinds
of parallelism.

00:50:59.530 --> 00:51:02.220
But you see with batch
size 8, as you increase

00:51:02.220 --> 00:51:05.070
the pipeline parallel size,
the number of devices,

00:51:05.070 --> 00:51:09.310
your utilization per GPU
starts to really drop off.

00:51:09.310 --> 00:51:11.740
Whereas if you have a big,
big batch size of 128,

00:51:11.740 --> 00:51:14.880
you can get away with
pretty good utilization

00:51:14.880 --> 00:51:17.730
for a reasonably sized
pipeline parallel.

00:51:17.730 --> 00:51:20.910
So batch sizes are really key to
hiding the size of the bubble.

00:51:20.910 --> 00:51:22.813
Otherwise, you have issues.

00:51:25.350 --> 00:51:31.720
Of course, you can do different
kinds of pipeline strategies.

00:51:31.720 --> 00:51:37.660
So instead of having
these the standard

00:51:37.660 --> 00:51:39.860
patterns for
scheduling the bubble.

00:51:39.860 --> 00:51:43.240
You can cut things
up into finer pieces

00:51:43.240 --> 00:51:46.552
where you're assigning
different stages, assigning

00:51:46.552 --> 00:51:48.260
different sublayers
to different devices,

00:51:48.260 --> 00:51:50.718
and you're doing different
computations at different parts.

00:51:50.718 --> 00:51:53.590
You can then interleave
the pipeline better.

00:51:53.590 --> 00:51:55.960
And an advanced
version of this that I

00:51:55.960 --> 00:51:57.920
want to spend a
moment talking about--

00:51:57.920 --> 00:52:01.570
and this is very, very clever,
is zero bubble pipelining--

00:52:01.570 --> 00:52:05.270
or I think in deep sea lingo,
I think they call it dual pipe.

00:52:05.270 --> 00:52:09.310
But the core single
trick is the same.

00:52:09.310 --> 00:52:12.550
So here, if you
think about it, let's

00:52:12.550 --> 00:52:15.790
say we're doing the backwards
pass to compute gradients.

00:52:15.790 --> 00:52:19.180
You can split this up into
two different components.

00:52:19.180 --> 00:52:23.950
The first part is about back
propagating the activations.

00:52:23.950 --> 00:52:27.260
So this is as I go down
the residual connections,

00:52:27.260 --> 00:52:30.130
I need to compute essentially
the derivative with respect

00:52:30.130 --> 00:52:31.190
to the activations.

00:52:31.190 --> 00:52:34.350
And then as I get
to a parameter,

00:52:34.350 --> 00:52:36.212
I also want to compute
the gradient itself.

00:52:36.212 --> 00:52:38.420
How am I going to update
the parameters, not just how

00:52:38.420 --> 00:52:42.000
do the activation change with
respect to the previous layers.

00:52:42.000 --> 00:52:43.800
And so to give you
a concrete example,

00:52:43.800 --> 00:52:46.320
let's look at this bottom
left diagram over here.

00:52:46.320 --> 00:52:49.260
So in this diagram, you
see the forward pass.

00:52:49.260 --> 00:52:50.640
This is a single MLP.

00:52:50.640 --> 00:52:53.360
So we've got
multiply by a weight.

00:52:53.360 --> 00:52:54.293
I do a non-linearity.

00:52:54.293 --> 00:52:56.460
And then I'm just going to
output the non-linearity.

00:52:56.460 --> 00:53:00.110
So this is a kind of a naive
single part of the MLP.

00:53:00.110 --> 00:53:01.740
Now let's look at the backwards.

00:53:01.740 --> 00:53:04.950
I have the derivative
with respect to the loss.

00:53:04.950 --> 00:53:05.850
It comes in.

00:53:05.850 --> 00:53:07.460
And then I can
compute how that's

00:53:07.460 --> 00:53:11.330
going to change the x's,
the inputs to my MLP.

00:53:11.330 --> 00:53:13.580
So this is, in some sense,
the derivative with respect

00:53:13.580 --> 00:53:15.060
to the activations here.

00:53:15.060 --> 00:53:17.070
And then as I compute
these, of course,

00:53:17.070 --> 00:53:19.700
I can use them to compute
the gradients that I

00:53:19.700 --> 00:53:20.970
need to update my weights.

00:53:20.970 --> 00:53:24.510
But the important thing
is this this part,

00:53:24.510 --> 00:53:27.030
this part of computing the
gradients for the weights.

00:53:27.030 --> 00:53:28.460
This can be done whenever.

00:53:28.460 --> 00:53:31.050
There's no dependence of this.

00:53:31.050 --> 00:53:34.350
And so I can rearrange the
scheduling for this computation

00:53:34.350 --> 00:53:36.610
to any part of the
computation graph.

00:53:36.610 --> 00:53:40.380
And so what you can do is you
can do your standard pipeline

00:53:40.380 --> 00:53:42.940
parallel for the parts that
are serially dependent.

00:53:42.940 --> 00:53:45.930
But any time you have to
do these computations just

00:53:45.930 --> 00:53:49.150
for updating the parameters, you
can reschedule them wherever.

00:53:49.150 --> 00:53:53.750
And so the key idea is you start
with a nice what's called 1F-1B

00:53:53.750 --> 00:53:54.250
pipeline.

00:53:54.250 --> 00:53:56.880
This is a nice optimized
reducing the bubble size

00:53:56.880 --> 00:53:58.110
schedule.

00:53:58.110 --> 00:53:59.920
And then you can take this.

00:53:59.920 --> 00:54:03.630
And what you can do is you
can separate this B, which

00:54:03.630 --> 00:54:06.250
is this computation
of the backwards part,

00:54:06.250 --> 00:54:09.690
and then W, which is the
computation necessary to compute

00:54:09.690 --> 00:54:11.050
the gradients of the weights.

00:54:11.050 --> 00:54:14.460
And now I can do the computation
of the weights, the W's, where

00:54:14.460 --> 00:54:16.210
I would have originally
had a bubble.

00:54:16.210 --> 00:54:21.630
So the parts where I originally
had these white idle utilization

00:54:21.630 --> 00:54:24.730
components, I can now fill
them in with these W's.

00:54:24.730 --> 00:54:26.820
And so by thinking
carefully about what

00:54:26.820 --> 00:54:29.370
the serial dependencies
actually are,

00:54:29.370 --> 00:54:31.810
I can now have
something really nice

00:54:31.810 --> 00:54:35.290
where I'm getting actually good
utilization out of my GPUs.

00:54:35.290 --> 00:54:39.730
To be clear, this is
horrendously complicated.

00:54:39.730 --> 00:54:42.370
If you actually want to
implement pipeline parallel

00:54:42.370 --> 00:54:43.930
in this way, you're
going to have

00:54:43.930 --> 00:54:48.670
to intervene in how your
autodiff is actually

00:54:48.670 --> 00:54:50.030
calculating these things.

00:54:50.030 --> 00:54:54.010
You have to have a queue that
can track where things go.

00:54:54.010 --> 00:54:56.650
I heard a funny anecdote
in a conversation

00:54:56.650 --> 00:55:00.110
recently from someone in a
frontier lab training LLMs,

00:55:00.110 --> 00:55:03.340
and they said, actually, there's
two people in the group that

00:55:03.340 --> 00:55:06.280
understand how the pipeline
parallel in our InfraWorks.

00:55:06.280 --> 00:55:07.210
One person left.

00:55:07.210 --> 00:55:08.710
And so there's a
single load bearing

00:55:08.710 --> 00:55:10.120
person in our training infra.

00:55:10.120 --> 00:55:12.820
There are stories like this.

00:55:12.820 --> 00:55:15.610
Pipeline parallel
is infrastructurally

00:55:15.610 --> 00:55:17.320
very, very complicated.

00:55:17.320 --> 00:55:18.760
It looks simple here.

00:55:18.760 --> 00:55:20.560
If you're interested,
I encourage

00:55:20.560 --> 00:55:22.360
you to try and implement it.

00:55:22.360 --> 00:55:25.270
It does get pretty
hairy pretty fast.

00:55:25.270 --> 00:55:28.000
And I think that's
a good note on which

00:55:28.000 --> 00:55:31.050
to switch to the other
model parallelism

00:55:31.050 --> 00:55:32.800
because this is much simpler.

00:55:32.800 --> 00:55:36.420
And this is often very cleanly
utilized by a lot of frameworks

00:55:36.420 --> 00:55:37.440
and a lot of--

00:55:37.440 --> 00:55:39.150
even people training
really big models

00:55:39.150 --> 00:55:41.220
rely very, very
heavily or primarily

00:55:41.220 --> 00:55:43.440
on this model parallelism.

00:55:43.440 --> 00:55:46.360
So what other way can
we split up a model?

00:55:46.360 --> 00:55:51.330
So if we think about it, most of
what we do is matrix multiplies.

00:55:51.330 --> 00:55:53.400
In a big model, most
of the computation

00:55:53.400 --> 00:55:54.430
is matrix multiplies.

00:55:54.430 --> 00:55:57.180
Most of the parameters are
matrix multiplies or matrices.

00:55:57.180 --> 00:55:58.660
And so what can we do?

00:55:58.660 --> 00:56:01.800
Well, if we can parallelize
just the matmuls,

00:56:01.800 --> 00:56:03.690
that would be pretty good.

00:56:03.690 --> 00:56:06.150
And so tensor
parallel is this idea

00:56:06.150 --> 00:56:09.390
that we can take a big matrix,
multiply and split it up

00:56:09.390 --> 00:56:12.300
into a set of submatrices
that can be multiplied.

00:56:12.300 --> 00:56:15.510
So if I have this matrix
multiply at the top--

00:56:15.510 --> 00:56:21.420
we have X and X times A equals
Y. What I can do instead

00:56:21.420 --> 00:56:24.990
is I can cut up into
half, and then I

00:56:24.990 --> 00:56:27.160
can also cut up X into half.

00:56:27.160 --> 00:56:30.270
And I can compute the
submatrices, I can sum them up,

00:56:30.270 --> 00:56:32.230
and then I will get
my answer at the end.

00:56:32.230 --> 00:56:34.680
So conceptually,
pipeline parallel

00:56:34.680 --> 00:56:37.920
is cutting along the depth
dimension like the layers.

00:56:37.920 --> 00:56:39.780
Tensor parallel,
which is what this is,

00:56:39.780 --> 00:56:42.810
is cutting up along the width
dimension of your matrix

00:56:42.810 --> 00:56:43.480
multiplies.

00:56:43.480 --> 00:56:45.900
And so we're going to
decompose into submatrices

00:56:45.900 --> 00:56:48.300
and then do partial sums.

00:56:48.300 --> 00:56:52.170
So here's an example of what
it might look like in an MLP.

00:56:52.170 --> 00:56:58.560
We have each GPU handling a
different submatrix of let's say

00:56:58.560 --> 00:57:00.420
a big MLP matrix multiply.

00:57:00.420 --> 00:57:02.670
And then we're going to have
collective communications

00:57:02.670 --> 00:57:06.670
to synchronize the
activations as we need them.

00:57:06.670 --> 00:57:08.830
So what are we going to do?

00:57:08.830 --> 00:57:10.290
So this is an MLP.

00:57:10.290 --> 00:57:12.623
And the top half and
the bottom half--

00:57:12.623 --> 00:57:13.790
there's two different paths.

00:57:13.790 --> 00:57:16.540
These are splitting
up the matrices.

00:57:16.540 --> 00:57:19.200
So I want to do this
operation Y equals

00:57:19.200 --> 00:57:22.920
GeLU X times A. I'm going
to split up my matrix

00:57:22.920 --> 00:57:24.610
A into A1 and A2.

00:57:24.610 --> 00:57:26.500
And then on the
right-hand side, I

00:57:26.500 --> 00:57:28.850
want to compute
dropout YB right,

00:57:28.850 --> 00:57:31.070
and then I want to
return the result as Z.

00:57:31.070 --> 00:57:33.130
So I'm going to also
cut up B. So I've

00:57:33.130 --> 00:57:36.490
cut up both of my big parameter
matrices into two parts,

00:57:36.490 --> 00:57:40.480
A and B. And in the forward
pass, what I'm going to do

00:57:40.480 --> 00:57:42.100
is I'm going to
take my inputs X,

00:57:42.100 --> 00:57:43.970
and I'm just going
to copy them twice.

00:57:43.970 --> 00:57:46.280
So each GPU is going
to get the same inputs.

00:57:46.280 --> 00:57:49.535
And they're going to operate
on it with A1 and A2.

00:57:49.535 --> 00:57:51.160
They have the same
kind of-- oh, sorry.

00:57:51.160 --> 00:57:53.690
They're the same row dimensions.

00:57:53.690 --> 00:57:56.050
So it's going to be
fine operating on them.

00:57:56.050 --> 00:58:00.500
So XA1 and XA2 is going to give
you some activations Y1 and Y2.

00:58:00.500 --> 00:58:02.180
Those are going to
go into B1 and B2.

00:58:02.180 --> 00:58:04.430
And then I'm going to do an
all reduce to sum them up.

00:58:04.430 --> 00:58:06.890
That's exactly the figure
I showed you before.

00:58:06.890 --> 00:58:09.010
So you copy, and
then you all reduce,

00:58:09.010 --> 00:58:10.900
and you get the answer Z.

00:58:10.900 --> 00:58:13.030
In the backwards pass,
now it's actually

00:58:13.030 --> 00:58:15.680
the reverse as the
gradients come backwards

00:58:15.680 --> 00:58:16.880
in the backward steps.

00:58:16.880 --> 00:58:20.270
This G is going to
be the identity.

00:58:20.270 --> 00:58:22.850
So I'm going to copy the
derivatives on both sides.

00:58:22.850 --> 00:58:24.650
And I'm going to do
the backwards operation

00:58:24.650 --> 00:58:25.590
all the way through.

00:58:25.590 --> 00:58:27.800
And once I get to
F, this is an all

00:58:27.800 --> 00:58:31.130
reduce, because I've got
two derivatives coming in

00:58:31.130 --> 00:58:32.130
from both paths.

00:58:32.130 --> 00:58:33.450
And then I sum them back up.

00:58:33.450 --> 00:58:35.810
So this F and G are
synchronization barriers.

00:58:35.810 --> 00:58:38.930
In the forward pass, I
do a single allreduce.

00:58:38.930 --> 00:58:41.240
On the backwards pass, I
do a single allreduce, just

00:58:41.240 --> 00:58:44.540
at two different places
in the computation graph.

00:58:44.540 --> 00:58:46.430
So now you can
hopefully see how this

00:58:46.430 --> 00:58:49.590
is a very nice way of wherever
you have a matrix multiply,

00:58:49.590 --> 00:58:51.470
you can just cut up
the matrix multiply

00:58:51.470 --> 00:58:54.600
and parallelize them
across different devices.

00:58:57.290 --> 00:59:00.480
And as you might imagine, this
is actually somewhat expensive.

00:59:00.480 --> 00:59:04.110
We have a synchronization
barrier that lives per layer.

00:59:04.110 --> 00:59:07.430
It needs to communicate
an activation

00:59:07.430 --> 00:59:11.210
the residual activation worth
of stuff twice in a forward

00:59:11.210 --> 00:59:12.270
backward pass.

00:59:12.270 --> 00:59:15.770
And so tensor parallel,
this very simple idea,

00:59:15.770 --> 00:59:18.988
is going to require very
high speed interconnects.

00:59:18.988 --> 00:59:20.280
And so there's a rule of thumb.

00:59:20.280 --> 00:59:21.980
It's a very simple rule
of thumb to remember

00:59:21.980 --> 00:59:23.522
which is that the
tensor parallel, is

00:59:23.522 --> 00:59:26.150
applied within a single node.

00:59:26.150 --> 00:59:30.100
So a single box of
let's say NVIDIA GPUs

00:59:30.100 --> 00:59:32.230
is going to ship with
eight different GPUs that

00:59:32.230 --> 00:59:33.350
live in that same box.

00:59:33.350 --> 00:59:36.620
And as I showed you at the
beginning of lecture today,

00:59:36.620 --> 00:59:38.510
they're very, very
high speed connected.

00:59:38.510 --> 00:59:41.860
Those eight GPUs can talk
to each other very quickly.

00:59:41.860 --> 00:59:44.200
And so it makes sense
to use something

00:59:44.200 --> 00:59:48.310
like tensor parallel that's
very bandwidth hungry

00:59:48.310 --> 00:59:49.820
between those eight devices.

00:59:49.820 --> 00:59:52.600
So what you will typically
see is that tensor parallel

00:59:52.600 --> 00:59:55.000
is applied up to eight GPUs
where the eight GPUs live

00:59:55.000 --> 00:59:57.880
in the same machine,
because that gives you

00:59:57.880 --> 00:59:59.630
the least drop in performance.

00:59:59.630 --> 01:00:01.750
And so this is an
example from Hugging

01:00:01.750 --> 01:00:04.990
faces parallelization
tutorial showing you

01:00:04.990 --> 01:00:07.870
the throughput decreases
of different levels

01:00:07.870 --> 01:00:09.550
of tensor parallelism.

01:00:09.550 --> 01:00:12.220
You see that there
are hits, 10% and 12%

01:00:12.220 --> 01:00:16.150
hits to throughput as you
do tensor parallelism.

01:00:16.150 --> 01:00:18.495
But up until 8, well,
maybe this is manageable.

01:00:18.495 --> 01:00:19.870
This is kind of
the price you pay

01:00:19.870 --> 01:00:22.180
for just being able to
parallelize more nicely.

01:00:22.180 --> 01:00:24.240
But then you go to
16 devices, and you

01:00:24.240 --> 01:00:28.270
get this kind of astounding
42% drop in performance.

01:00:28.270 --> 01:00:32.850
You go to 32, and you see
another 65% drop in throughput.

01:00:32.850 --> 01:00:34.680
And so you see,
hopefully visually here,

01:00:34.680 --> 01:00:37.420
that you really want to stop
at 8 for tensor parallelism.

01:00:37.420 --> 01:00:40.200
That's really the sweet spot,
because of the kinds of hardware

01:00:40.200 --> 01:00:44.444
interconnects you can
get your hands on.

01:00:44.444 --> 01:00:48.360
So how do things now compare
to pipelined parallel?

01:00:48.360 --> 01:00:50.160
Well, compared to
pipeline parallel,

01:00:50.160 --> 01:00:52.650
we don't really have to
deal with this bubble thing

01:00:52.650 --> 01:00:53.740
that we had before.

01:00:53.740 --> 01:00:56.970
We don't need to consume
larger batch sizes in order

01:00:56.970 --> 01:00:59.410
to reduce the bubble,
which is nice.

01:00:59.410 --> 01:01:01.500
And there's
relatively-- I wouldn't

01:01:01.500 --> 01:01:04.530
say very-- there's relatively
low complexity in applying

01:01:04.530 --> 01:01:05.380
tensor parallel.

01:01:05.380 --> 01:01:06.780
All you really
need to know about

01:01:06.780 --> 01:01:09.070
are where are the big
matrix multiplies, can I

01:01:09.070 --> 01:01:11.320
split them up and make them
live on different devices.

01:01:11.320 --> 01:01:13.470
The forwards and
backwards operations still

01:01:13.470 --> 01:01:14.460
remain the same.

01:01:14.460 --> 01:01:16.200
Compared to
implementing something

01:01:16.200 --> 01:01:19.840
like zero overhead or
dual pipeline parallel,

01:01:19.840 --> 01:01:23.700
you're going to be in much,
much better shape doing this.

01:01:23.700 --> 01:01:27.630
So the con is that it's much
larger communication overhead.

01:01:27.630 --> 01:01:29.850
You've got, in
pipeline parallel,

01:01:29.850 --> 01:01:33.240
batch size times sequence length
times residual dimension, point

01:01:33.240 --> 01:01:35.670
to point communications
per micro-batch.

01:01:35.670 --> 01:01:39.660
In tensor parallel, you've got
eight times that per layer,

01:01:39.660 --> 01:01:41.760
and you've got all
reduced communication.

01:01:41.760 --> 01:01:44.460
It's potentially a very
large amount of communication

01:01:44.460 --> 01:01:45.720
that needs to be done.

01:01:45.720 --> 01:01:48.280
So the rule of thumb,
as I said before,

01:01:48.280 --> 01:01:51.490
is tensor parallel is used
whenever you have low latency,

01:01:51.490 --> 01:01:52.960
high bandwidth interconnects.

01:01:52.960 --> 01:01:56.190
You're going to see
2 to 16, depending

01:01:56.190 --> 01:01:59.730
on what kinds of machines you
have of tensor parallel out

01:01:59.730 --> 01:02:00.430
in the wild.

01:02:00.430 --> 01:02:02.760
And I'll show you
examples as I talk

01:02:02.760 --> 01:02:06.570
through at the very end here
of examples of tensor parallel.

01:02:06.570 --> 01:02:09.930
Any questions on
pipeline or tensor

01:02:09.930 --> 01:02:13.380
parallel before we move on to
the kind of third kind, sequence

01:02:13.380 --> 01:02:15.760
parallel and
activation sharding?

01:02:15.760 --> 01:02:16.330
Yes.

01:02:16.330 --> 01:02:18.110
[INAUDIBLE]

01:02:22.330 --> 01:02:24.850
Yeah, so the question was, can
they be used simultaneously.

01:02:24.850 --> 01:02:27.980
The answer is that, yeah,
you do use them both.

01:02:27.980 --> 01:02:30.617
So I think we'll get
to examples later.

01:02:30.617 --> 01:02:32.200
But I think the
typical thing that you

01:02:32.200 --> 01:02:37.750
see is for large scale runs, you
very often see tensor parallel.

01:02:37.750 --> 01:02:40.400
Pipeline parallel is
often used on top of that.

01:02:40.400 --> 01:02:44.140
I think the only example I
know of that does pipeline,

01:02:44.140 --> 01:02:48.830
but not tensor parallel would be
DeepSeek, V3, as far as I know.

01:02:48.830 --> 01:02:50.590
So within a single
machine, I guess

01:02:50.590 --> 01:02:53.650
you have-- say if you have
five different machines,

01:02:53.650 --> 01:02:55.510
maybe the first 20%
of the parameters

01:02:55.510 --> 01:02:59.440
are across the reach of
the first machine, which

01:02:59.440 --> 01:03:00.710
you use tensor parallel for.

01:03:00.710 --> 01:03:03.242
And then that pipeline parallels
into the second machine

01:03:03.242 --> 01:03:04.450
where you have the next step.

01:03:04.450 --> 01:03:06.607
Yeah, so the
question was there--

01:03:06.607 --> 01:03:09.190
do you do tensor parallel within
machine and pipeline parallel

01:03:09.190 --> 01:03:10.315
across machine for example.

01:03:10.315 --> 01:03:12.760
Yeah, so you would do
something like tensor parallel

01:03:12.760 --> 01:03:15.430
within machine and a combination
of data and pipeline parallel

01:03:15.430 --> 01:03:16.950
across machines for example.

01:03:16.950 --> 01:03:19.200
And I'll show you the
rule of thumb later.

01:03:19.200 --> 01:03:21.470
But basically, you
do pipeline parallel

01:03:21.470 --> 01:03:23.340
because your models won't fit.

01:03:23.340 --> 01:03:25.410
If you could fit
your entire model,

01:03:25.410 --> 01:03:28.340
you just do data parallel
plus tensor parallel or just

01:03:28.340 --> 01:03:31.100
maybe even data parallel.

01:03:31.100 --> 01:03:33.050
Great, OK.

01:03:33.050 --> 01:03:33.710
Excellent.

01:03:33.710 --> 01:03:36.060
So then we've been
talking about memory.

01:03:36.060 --> 01:03:38.690
And memory is, in some
sense, a very important part

01:03:38.690 --> 01:03:42.350
of parallelization because we're
going to be training big models.

01:03:42.350 --> 01:03:45.180
And so when you
look at your memory,

01:03:45.180 --> 01:03:47.630
you realize that,
actually, activations

01:03:47.630 --> 01:03:49.800
are a really big part
of your memory usage.

01:03:49.800 --> 01:03:53.910
So if you look at standard
kind of forward backward pass,

01:03:53.910 --> 01:03:56.900
I think this was from one
of the PyTorch tutorials,

01:03:56.900 --> 01:03:59.810
you see that memory
usage is very dynamic.

01:03:59.810 --> 01:04:02.360
So I'll just talk through
this, because I think it's

01:04:02.360 --> 01:04:03.690
an interesting plot in general.

01:04:03.690 --> 01:04:06.290
You always have your
parameters as you're

01:04:06.290 --> 01:04:08.220
training because that's static.

01:04:08.220 --> 01:04:11.030
But in iteration
zero, you don't still

01:04:11.030 --> 01:04:12.445
have optimizer state at all.

01:04:12.445 --> 01:04:14.820
So actually, you don't have
that part of your memory use.

01:04:14.820 --> 01:04:17.140
But as you do your
forward and backward, you

01:04:17.140 --> 01:04:19.150
see activation gross,
gross, gross, gross,

01:04:19.150 --> 01:04:22.160
grows as you accumulate
all the activations.

01:04:22.160 --> 01:04:24.165
And as you start
your backwards pass,

01:04:24.165 --> 01:04:26.290
your activation goes down
because you're freeing it

01:04:26.290 --> 01:04:28.630
as you use up your
activations, and then you're

01:04:28.630 --> 01:04:29.870
accumulating your gradients.

01:04:29.870 --> 01:04:31.600
So your gradient
memory usage goes up.

01:04:31.600 --> 01:04:34.330
And the peak is actually
somewhere partially

01:04:34.330 --> 01:04:35.740
through your
backwards pass where

01:04:35.740 --> 01:04:38.110
you haven't freed all
your activations yet,

01:04:38.110 --> 01:04:40.130
and you're still building
up your gradients.

01:04:40.130 --> 01:04:42.940
So in iteration two, you
see the same thing here.

01:04:42.940 --> 01:04:46.252
So the point of this
diagram is to say, well,

01:04:46.252 --> 01:04:47.960
we've thought about
all the other pieces.

01:04:47.960 --> 01:04:49.543
We thought about the
parameters, we've

01:04:49.543 --> 01:04:51.100
thought about
optimizer state, we've

01:04:51.100 --> 01:04:53.260
thought about the gradients.

01:04:53.260 --> 01:04:55.840
But we have not thought
about, very deeply at least,

01:04:55.840 --> 01:04:56.990
the activations.

01:04:56.990 --> 01:04:58.670
And so let's do that.

01:04:58.670 --> 01:05:01.420
So the final complexity that
I want to talk you through

01:05:01.420 --> 01:05:03.970
is the activation memory.

01:05:03.970 --> 01:05:06.460
So tensor and pipeline
parallel can linearly

01:05:06.460 --> 01:05:08.330
reduce basically most things.

01:05:08.330 --> 01:05:12.740
But it can't actually reduce all
of the activation memory usage.

01:05:12.740 --> 01:05:16.210
And so this is an example
from one of the NVIDIA papers

01:05:16.210 --> 01:05:19.330
that's talking about how do
you reduce activation memory.

01:05:19.330 --> 01:05:21.580
And I think one thing that's
really interesting to see

01:05:21.580 --> 01:05:23.240
is that you make your
models bigger and bigger.

01:05:23.240 --> 01:05:24.440
So going from left to right.

01:05:24.440 --> 01:05:27.280
You see that parameter
and optimizer state

01:05:27.280 --> 01:05:30.860
memory can remain the same if
we parallelize aggressively.

01:05:30.860 --> 01:05:33.130
But activation memory
just kind of continues

01:05:33.130 --> 01:05:36.470
to grow because some parts of it
don't parallelize very cleanly.

01:05:36.470 --> 01:05:38.860
So no matter the number
of devices you have,

01:05:38.860 --> 01:05:40.630
actually you can't
really get rid

01:05:40.630 --> 01:05:42.950
of the growth of activation
memory per device.

01:05:42.950 --> 01:05:44.633
And I'll show you
why in a moment here.

01:05:44.633 --> 01:05:47.050
Whereas I think if you do some
slightly more clever things

01:05:47.050 --> 01:05:51.140
like recomputation, you can
keep the activation memory low.

01:05:51.140 --> 01:05:52.925
And that's really
key to parallelizing

01:05:52.925 --> 01:05:54.050
some of the biggest models.

01:05:57.220 --> 01:06:00.530
So what's the activation
memory per layer?

01:06:00.530 --> 01:06:04.120
You've kind of done some of this
transformer math and calculus

01:06:04.120 --> 01:06:04.670
before.

01:06:04.670 --> 01:06:08.165
So hopefully, you're now
familiar with all of this.

01:06:08.165 --> 01:06:10.540
But we can compute what's the
amount of activation memory

01:06:10.540 --> 01:06:11.570
we need per layer.

01:06:11.570 --> 01:06:13.140
And there's a
handy formula here.

01:06:13.140 --> 01:06:14.890
And this is the amount
of memory you need.

01:06:14.890 --> 01:06:19.400
It's sbh times 34
plus 5 as over h.

01:06:19.400 --> 01:06:21.150
And some of these
numbers are mystifying,

01:06:21.150 --> 01:06:23.570
but actually they're
not so mystifying.

01:06:23.570 --> 01:06:26.750
You can very much see
that there's a left term

01:06:26.750 --> 01:06:28.050
and then there's a right term.

01:06:28.050 --> 01:06:33.480
The left term comes from the MLP
and other pointwise operations.

01:06:33.480 --> 01:06:35.760
That's where sbh
times 34 comes from.

01:06:35.760 --> 01:06:40.010
These depend on the size of
your residual stream, the h.

01:06:40.010 --> 01:06:41.540
On the right side,
you have a term

01:06:41.540 --> 01:06:44.120
that's actually, if you
multiply this out, as squared

01:06:44.120 --> 01:06:46.460
b because the h is cancel.

01:06:46.460 --> 01:06:49.730
That's the memory that you
need for the softmax term

01:06:49.730 --> 01:06:52.560
and other quadratic
terms in your attention.

01:06:52.560 --> 01:06:59.377
Of course, if you use flash
attention and use recomputation,

01:06:59.377 --> 01:07:01.710
we know that we can drastically
reduce that second term.

01:07:01.710 --> 01:07:06.180
So then let's say we
do tensor parallel.

01:07:06.180 --> 01:07:07.860
We do tensor parallel
everywhere we can.

01:07:07.860 --> 01:07:12.180
So we do it in the MLPs, we
do it in the kq computations,

01:07:12.180 --> 01:07:16.230
in the attention computation.

01:07:16.230 --> 01:07:18.940
We will end up with something
that looks like this.

01:07:18.940 --> 01:07:21.670
And this is looking pretty
good, but not quite there.

01:07:21.670 --> 01:07:23.880
So activation memory
per layer divided

01:07:23.880 --> 01:07:28.560
by t, which is the number
of devices that were tensor

01:07:28.560 --> 01:07:29.590
paralleling over.

01:07:29.590 --> 01:07:32.460
So if we're dividing
by 8, ideally, we

01:07:32.460 --> 01:07:34.920
would divide all the
activation memory by 8.

01:07:34.920 --> 01:07:38.640
But you see there's this
straggler term, sbh times 10,

01:07:38.640 --> 01:07:41.850
that has not been reduced down.

01:07:41.850 --> 01:07:44.460
And if you think
about what these are,

01:07:44.460 --> 01:07:46.570
these are the non
matmul components.

01:07:46.570 --> 01:07:48.960
So the LayerNorm, the
Dropouts, the inputs

01:07:48.960 --> 01:07:50.580
to the attention, and the MLP--

01:07:50.580 --> 01:07:53.430
all of these terms
will unfortunately

01:07:53.430 --> 01:07:55.590
continue to grow with
size and they will not

01:07:55.590 --> 01:07:57.190
be parallelized very nicely.

01:07:57.190 --> 01:08:02.010
And so the very last thing
that we need to think about

01:08:02.010 --> 01:08:04.500
is to take those simple
pointwise operations,

01:08:04.500 --> 01:08:07.290
which thus far, we
have not parallelized,

01:08:07.290 --> 01:08:08.930
and we just need
to split them up.

01:08:08.930 --> 01:08:11.870
And there's a very simple
way to split them up,

01:08:11.870 --> 01:08:14.890
which is to say, well, if
we're doing a layer norm,

01:08:14.890 --> 01:08:17.319
these layer norms across
different positions

01:08:17.319 --> 01:08:20.109
in the sequence do not interact
at all with each other.

01:08:20.109 --> 01:08:23.380
They just don't care
about anything else.

01:08:23.380 --> 01:08:26.350
And so what we're going to do
is let's say we have a 10 24

01:08:26.350 --> 01:08:28.520
long sequence, we're
going to cut that up,

01:08:28.520 --> 01:08:32.109
and then each device will handle
a different part of that layer

01:08:32.109 --> 01:08:33.920
norm, or different
part of that dropout.

01:08:33.920 --> 01:08:36.729
Those pointwise operations
can now be completely split up

01:08:36.729 --> 01:08:38.859
across the sequence dimension.

01:08:38.859 --> 01:08:41.950
And because now we're cutting
things up across the sequence

01:08:41.950 --> 01:08:44.529
dimension, we're going to have
to do some synchronization

01:08:44.529 --> 01:08:47.649
to make sure the
parallel computations

01:08:47.649 --> 01:08:50.350
that we did can get
aggregated back again.

01:08:50.350 --> 01:08:52.270
And so in the forward
pass these guys,

01:08:52.270 --> 01:08:54.520
they're going to be
all gathers and g bars

01:08:54.520 --> 01:08:55.970
are going to be
reduced scatters.

01:08:55.970 --> 01:08:58.250
And in the backwards pass,
the two are reversed.

01:08:58.250 --> 01:09:01.660
In some sense, they're a
duality here between the two.

01:09:01.660 --> 01:09:04.100
And what we're doing here
is for the layer norm,

01:09:04.100 --> 01:09:06.442
we've kind of scattered
things around.

01:09:06.442 --> 01:09:08.359
And so we're going to
have to gather them back

01:09:08.359 --> 01:09:11.696
together so that we can do
of our standard computation.

01:09:11.696 --> 01:09:13.529
And then now whenever
we get to the dropout,

01:09:13.529 --> 01:09:16.670
we want to scatter them back
out into the parallel component

01:09:16.670 --> 01:09:17.220
that we have.

01:09:17.220 --> 01:09:18.762
And in the backwards
pass, we're kind

01:09:18.762 --> 01:09:21.229
of doing that in the reverse.

01:09:21.229 --> 01:09:24.380
So hopefully, that is clear.

01:09:24.380 --> 01:09:25.939
This is a very simple idea.

01:09:25.939 --> 01:09:28.760
We're just parallelizing
the very last components

01:09:28.760 --> 01:09:30.979
that we failed to
parallelize before.

01:09:30.979 --> 01:09:33.740
And so now, we can put all
of these different pieces

01:09:33.740 --> 01:09:37.710
together and get to the end,
which is we started up here,

01:09:37.710 --> 01:09:39.210
which is no parallelism at all.

01:09:39.210 --> 01:09:41.120
We did tensor parallel,
which allows us

01:09:41.120 --> 01:09:44.520
to divide everything that's
not a pointwise op by t.

01:09:44.520 --> 01:09:48.090
And then if we apply this
sequence parallelism idea,

01:09:48.090 --> 01:09:51.470
we can divide this
component by t once more.

01:09:51.470 --> 01:09:54.620
And then we can do things
like activation recomputation

01:09:54.620 --> 01:09:56.390
which is the flash
attention trick,

01:09:56.390 --> 01:09:57.990
to remove the second term.

01:09:57.990 --> 01:10:00.600
And the minimal memory
that you can easily

01:10:00.600 --> 01:10:02.850
get away with is going to
be this thing on the bottom,

01:10:02.850 --> 01:10:05.620
which is sbh 34 over t.

01:10:05.620 --> 01:10:08.320
And this is often
used if you're looking

01:10:08.320 --> 01:10:10.450
at different formulas for
transformer arithmetic

01:10:10.450 --> 01:10:12.880
on how much activation
memory do I use.

01:10:12.880 --> 01:10:15.670
You often see something
like sbh 34 and then

01:10:15.670 --> 01:10:18.250
if you have t tensor
parallel divided by t,

01:10:18.250 --> 01:10:20.950
because this is the easy
minimum that you can

01:10:20.950 --> 01:10:23.380
get for that kind of a memory.

01:10:23.380 --> 01:10:28.400
Any questions on sequence
parallel and activations?

01:10:28.400 --> 01:10:29.200
Yes.

01:10:29.200 --> 01:10:32.020
There's one more thing, as
the transformers stack up

01:10:32.020 --> 01:10:34.240
each other, I suppose
the computational graph

01:10:34.240 --> 01:10:37.720
will grow more and more
like model [INAUDIBLE]

01:10:37.720 --> 01:10:41.470
computational graph
as a [INAUDIBLE] ever

01:10:41.470 --> 01:10:45.100
become a bottleneck in the
communication between the GPUs?

01:10:45.100 --> 01:10:46.840
You're saying if we
have something that's

01:10:46.840 --> 01:10:48.880
a more complicated
computation graph

01:10:48.880 --> 01:10:53.200
than a single linear chain,
will that become a problem?

01:10:53.200 --> 01:10:54.680
It's a good question.

01:10:54.680 --> 01:10:56.120
I haven't thought about that.

01:10:56.120 --> 01:10:57.530
I would guess not.

01:10:57.530 --> 01:11:00.350
At least for tensor parallel,
this operates purely layer wise.

01:11:00.350 --> 01:11:01.970
It doesn't really care
about the dependencies.

01:11:01.970 --> 01:11:03.470
Maybe for pipeline
parallel, there's

01:11:03.470 --> 01:11:05.600
opportunities for
increased parallelization

01:11:05.600 --> 01:11:06.960
if there's more than one branch.

01:11:06.960 --> 01:11:08.300
But I'm not too sure.

01:11:08.300 --> 01:11:10.760
[INAUDIBLE]

01:11:17.480 --> 01:11:19.910
Yes, right.

01:11:19.910 --> 01:11:21.810
OK, cool.

01:11:21.810 --> 01:11:22.440
All right.

01:11:22.440 --> 01:11:25.550
So there's a few other
parallelism strategies

01:11:25.550 --> 01:11:28.640
that I'm not going to
talk about just because

01:11:28.640 --> 01:11:32.493
in the interest of
time and fatiguing you,

01:11:32.493 --> 01:11:34.160
because I think I've
already dragged you

01:11:34.160 --> 01:11:36.200
through a whole bunch
of low level details

01:11:36.200 --> 01:11:38.600
about how to do parallelization.

01:11:38.600 --> 01:11:40.550
So the first one I
want to talk about

01:11:40.550 --> 01:11:43.410
is context parallel
or ring attention.

01:11:43.410 --> 01:11:45.710
You may have heard the
term ring attention before.

01:11:45.710 --> 01:11:48.440
This is a way of
essentially splitting up

01:11:48.440 --> 01:11:51.350
both the computation
and the activation cost

01:11:51.350 --> 01:11:54.020
of computing really
large attention,

01:11:54.020 --> 01:11:57.830
where essentially, you're just
going to passkeys and values

01:11:57.830 --> 01:11:59.340
around different machines.

01:11:59.340 --> 01:12:02.070
So each machine is responsible
for a different query,

01:12:02.070 --> 01:12:03.560
and then keys and
values are going

01:12:03.560 --> 01:12:07.250
to travel from machine to
machine in a ring like fashion

01:12:07.250 --> 01:12:10.740
in order to compute
your KQV inner products.

01:12:10.740 --> 01:12:12.470
And the cool thing
here is you already

01:12:12.470 --> 01:12:14.510
know how to do this
because you've done

01:12:14.510 --> 01:12:17.570
the tiling for flash attention.

01:12:17.570 --> 01:12:19.280
So you know that
attention can be

01:12:19.280 --> 01:12:22.290
computed in this kind of
online tile by tile way.

01:12:22.290 --> 01:12:25.070
And that's what's happening
in ring attention.

01:12:25.070 --> 01:12:28.100
The other thing, which now
that you know tensor parallel

01:12:28.100 --> 01:12:30.900
is pretty straightforward,
is expert parallelism.

01:12:30.900 --> 01:12:34.340
Expert parallelism, you can
think of as almost like tensor

01:12:34.340 --> 01:12:36.740
parallel in the sense
that you're splitting up

01:12:36.740 --> 01:12:41.045
one big MLP into smaller
expert MLPs, let's say,

01:12:41.045 --> 01:12:43.170
and then scattering them
across different machines.

01:12:43.170 --> 01:12:45.170
The key difference
with expert parallelism

01:12:45.170 --> 01:12:47.753
is that the experts
are sparsely activated.

01:12:47.753 --> 01:12:49.920
And so you have to think a
little bit about routing.

01:12:49.920 --> 01:12:54.180
And the routing is not
going to be as predictable,

01:12:54.180 --> 01:12:56.510
let's say, as the all
to all communication

01:12:56.510 --> 01:12:58.830
that we had before
in tensor parallel,

01:12:58.830 --> 01:13:01.248
because now maybe one
expert is overloaded.

01:13:01.248 --> 01:13:03.790
Your networking is going to be
a little bit more complicated.

01:13:03.790 --> 01:13:05.220
But otherwise,
conceptually you're

01:13:05.220 --> 01:13:08.640
living in the same world as
tensor parallel for expert

01:13:08.640 --> 01:13:09.580
parallelism.

01:13:12.882 --> 01:13:16.560
So just to recap all the
things we talked about,

01:13:16.560 --> 01:13:19.410
I've made a little small
table of the different kinds

01:13:19.410 --> 01:13:21.120
of strategies that we have.

01:13:21.120 --> 01:13:23.640
We have DDP in ZeRO 1.

01:13:23.640 --> 01:13:26.820
This is kind of the naive data
parallelism thing that you do.

01:13:26.820 --> 01:13:29.620
Here, you have some
overhead per batch,

01:13:29.620 --> 01:13:33.360
you have no memory scaling,
reasonable bandwidth properties,

01:13:33.360 --> 01:13:36.640
but you consume batch size in
order to be able to do this.

01:13:36.640 --> 01:13:39.480
You need big batch sizes to
have big data parallelism.

01:13:39.480 --> 01:13:43.560
You have FSDP, which is kind
of a nicer version of ZeRO 1

01:13:43.560 --> 01:13:46.450
in the sense that you
can get memory scaling,

01:13:46.450 --> 01:13:50.490
but you're going to pay overhead
across different layers.

01:13:50.490 --> 01:13:53.580
And so now you've got
higher communication costs,

01:13:53.580 --> 01:13:55.950
and you've got potentially
synchronization barriers

01:13:55.950 --> 01:13:57.760
that lead to poor utilization.

01:13:57.760 --> 01:14:03.010
Pipeline parallel is nice
in that we no longer have

01:14:03.010 --> 01:14:06.920
this dependence on
this per batch aspects,

01:14:06.920 --> 01:14:09.290
and we can get linear
memory scaling,

01:14:09.290 --> 01:14:12.830
but we have another issue, which
is this also consumes batch size

01:14:12.830 --> 01:14:15.200
and it's horrendous
to set up and use.

01:14:15.200 --> 01:14:17.800
And so a lot of people like
to avoid pipeline parallelism

01:14:17.800 --> 01:14:18.980
if it's possible.

01:14:18.980 --> 01:14:20.710
And then finally,
tensor parallelism

01:14:20.710 --> 01:14:23.050
is very high cost in
terms of bandwidth

01:14:23.050 --> 01:14:26.440
and the amount of
synchronization you need to do.

01:14:26.440 --> 01:14:29.170
But this has this really
nice property that

01:14:29.170 --> 01:14:30.950
has no impact on batch sizes.

01:14:30.950 --> 01:14:32.680
So it's kind one
parallelism strategy

01:14:32.680 --> 01:14:36.220
you can use that has no cost
in terms of your global batch

01:14:36.220 --> 01:14:37.520
size, which is nice.

01:14:37.520 --> 01:14:40.730
So we have to balance a
number of limited resources.

01:14:40.730 --> 01:14:43.040
We have memory, which
is one resource.

01:14:43.040 --> 01:14:46.340
We have bandwidth and compute,
which is another resource.

01:14:46.340 --> 01:14:48.100
And then we have
batch size, which

01:14:48.100 --> 01:14:50.140
is kind of an unconventional
resource, but one

01:14:50.140 --> 01:14:52.223
that you should really
think of as a limited thing

01:14:52.223 --> 01:14:54.730
that you can spend on
different aspects of these

01:14:54.730 --> 01:14:57.650
to improve your efficiency.

01:14:57.650 --> 01:15:00.950
And there's a very
nice TPU parallelism,

01:15:00.950 --> 01:15:03.200
or TPU book, let's
call it, from Google

01:15:03.200 --> 01:15:05.700
that I referred to last week.

01:15:05.700 --> 01:15:08.337
But also actually, they have a
really nice parallelism section.

01:15:08.337 --> 01:15:09.920
And they have this
great figure that I

01:15:09.920 --> 01:15:11.840
wanted to show
you before I moved

01:15:11.840 --> 01:15:13.820
on to some of the examples.

01:15:13.820 --> 01:15:16.560
So the key quantity,
as I was saying before,

01:15:16.560 --> 01:15:17.820
is the batch size.

01:15:17.820 --> 01:15:21.920
And depending on the ratio of
batch size to the number of GPUs

01:15:21.920 --> 01:15:25.980
you have, different kinds of
parallelism become optimal.

01:15:25.980 --> 01:15:29.570
And so they use certain formula
on how much communication

01:15:29.570 --> 01:15:33.690
and computation you end up
doing for each of these models.

01:15:33.690 --> 01:15:36.990
So this is a simplified
formula to generate this plot.

01:15:36.990 --> 01:15:40.470
And you can see if your
batch size is too small,

01:15:40.470 --> 01:15:45.320
you have lots of GPUs and really
tiny batch sizes, then there

01:15:45.320 --> 01:15:47.540
is no way for you
to be efficient.

01:15:47.540 --> 01:15:48.958
You're always
communication bound,

01:15:48.958 --> 01:15:50.250
which is this bottom half here.

01:15:50.250 --> 01:15:51.890
And in fact, you're
spending most

01:15:51.890 --> 01:15:53.840
of your time on communication.

01:15:53.840 --> 01:15:56.230
As you get more and
more batch size,

01:15:56.230 --> 01:15:59.820
eventually you can get to
a point where if you mix

01:15:59.820 --> 01:16:00.660
both FSDP--

01:16:00.660 --> 01:16:04.720
so ZeRO stage 3-- and MP, which
in this case is tensor parallel,

01:16:04.720 --> 01:16:06.948
you can actually get
basically to a place

01:16:06.948 --> 01:16:07.990
where your compute bound.

01:16:07.990 --> 01:16:13.960
So now you're not wasting your
flops waiting for communication.

01:16:13.960 --> 01:16:16.260
And then finally, if
you get to a point

01:16:16.260 --> 01:16:18.600
where your batch
sizes are big, then

01:16:18.600 --> 01:16:21.990
you can just get away
with pure data parallel.

01:16:21.990 --> 01:16:25.260
Pure FSDP is going to
get you into a regime

01:16:25.260 --> 01:16:28.260
where the time you
spend doing computation

01:16:28.260 --> 01:16:30.820
is higher than the time you
spend doing communication.

01:16:30.820 --> 01:16:32.320
So if your batch
size is big enough,

01:16:32.320 --> 01:16:34.230
you can just get away with FSDP.

01:16:34.230 --> 01:16:36.690
So this is a cool
illustration of this idea

01:16:36.690 --> 01:16:39.760
of why would you mix these,
when would you mix these,

01:16:39.760 --> 01:16:41.380
why is batch size of resource.

01:16:41.380 --> 01:16:42.755
Hopefully, this
kind of shows you

01:16:42.755 --> 01:16:46.680
in a very visual
way what this is.

01:16:46.680 --> 01:16:48.460
And so when you put
these all together,

01:16:48.460 --> 01:16:51.960
you end up with what people
call 3D or 4D parallelism.

01:16:51.960 --> 01:16:54.890
I think I've heard the term
5d parallelism recently.

01:16:54.890 --> 01:16:58.110
I wasn't quite sure what
the fifth dimension was yet.

01:16:58.110 --> 01:16:59.750
I'll have to read up on that.

01:16:59.750 --> 01:17:01.370
But now you can
put it all together

01:17:01.370 --> 01:17:03.330
the different dimensions
of parallelism.

01:17:03.330 --> 01:17:06.230
And this is a really
simple rule of thumb.

01:17:06.230 --> 01:17:09.150
I originally looked it up and
put this together last year,

01:17:09.150 --> 01:17:11.340
but turns out it's still
the same this year.

01:17:11.340 --> 01:17:13.860
So you can follow this now.

01:17:13.860 --> 01:17:16.250
So the first thing
you have to do

01:17:16.250 --> 01:17:19.070
is you have to fit your model
and your activations in memory.

01:17:19.070 --> 01:17:21.150
If you don't do that,
you just cannot train.

01:17:21.150 --> 01:17:22.340
So this is a requirement.

01:17:22.340 --> 01:17:24.020
So until your model
fits in memory,

01:17:24.020 --> 01:17:25.620
we have to split up our model.

01:17:25.620 --> 01:17:27.510
So we're going to do
tensor parallelism.

01:17:27.510 --> 01:17:30.073
And we know that up to the
number of GPUs per machine

01:17:30.073 --> 01:17:31.740
that's very efficient,
that's very fast.

01:17:31.740 --> 01:17:33.990
So we're going to do tensor
parallel up to that point.

01:17:33.990 --> 01:17:37.520
Now after that, depending
on things like your desire

01:17:37.520 --> 01:17:41.330
to deal with pipeline
parallel and/or your bandwidth

01:17:41.330 --> 01:17:44.720
constraints, you're either
going to use ZeRO 3 or pipeline

01:17:44.720 --> 01:17:47.210
parallel across the
machines until you

01:17:47.210 --> 01:17:48.690
can fit your model in memory.

01:17:48.690 --> 01:17:53.140
Now, after that point, well,
until you run out of GPUs,

01:17:53.140 --> 01:17:55.770
you can now run the whole
thing, and your only goal

01:17:55.770 --> 01:17:57.810
is to increase the
amount of total flops

01:17:57.810 --> 01:17:58.930
that you have on hand.

01:17:58.930 --> 01:18:00.763
So you're going to scale
the rest of the way

01:18:00.763 --> 01:18:03.360
with data parallel,
because data parallel is--

01:18:03.360 --> 01:18:06.970
it works well on low bandwidth
communication channels

01:18:06.970 --> 01:18:08.710
and it is very simple.

01:18:08.710 --> 01:18:14.190
And so that's going to give you
a way of using all of your GPUs.

01:18:14.190 --> 01:18:16.780
Now if your batch
size is really small,

01:18:16.780 --> 01:18:21.690
then there is a way of
trading batch sizes for better

01:18:21.690 --> 01:18:22.840
communication efficiency.

01:18:22.840 --> 01:18:25.050
If you haven't consumed
all of your batch size

01:18:25.050 --> 01:18:27.120
as a resource,
what you can do is

01:18:27.120 --> 01:18:30.540
you can use gradient
accumulation on your devices.

01:18:30.540 --> 01:18:34.620
And that will let you basically
have effectively larger batch

01:18:34.620 --> 01:18:36.518
sizes even if you're
memory constrained.

01:18:36.518 --> 01:18:38.310
And that will let you
trade your batch size

01:18:38.310 --> 01:18:39.900
for better
communication efficiency

01:18:39.900 --> 01:18:44.050
since you're synchronizing
less often across machines.

01:18:44.050 --> 01:18:45.120
Simple rule of thumb.

01:18:45.120 --> 01:18:48.300
This will let you train models
with reasonable efficiency

01:18:48.300 --> 01:18:50.190
no matter what you're doing.

01:18:50.190 --> 01:18:52.480
And so to make
this concrete, I'll

01:18:52.480 --> 01:18:55.160
talk through a few examples
at the very end here.

01:18:55.160 --> 01:18:59.950
I'll flash through both this
really lovely paper back in 2021

01:18:59.950 --> 01:19:02.080
from Megatron LLM
basically showing you

01:19:02.080 --> 01:19:04.990
exactly these things
in pictures, and also

01:19:04.990 --> 01:19:07.450
a lot of ablations
as well as some

01:19:07.450 --> 01:19:09.100
of the models from last year.

01:19:09.100 --> 01:19:11.200
So this is a big
table of how they

01:19:11.200 --> 01:19:14.710
trained models going from
1.7 billion parameters

01:19:14.710 --> 01:19:16.510
to 1 trillion parameters.

01:19:16.510 --> 01:19:19.400
And they get great
utilization on all of these.

01:19:19.400 --> 01:19:22.270
You see percentage of
theoretical peak flops

01:19:22.270 --> 01:19:25.250
that they get and it
ranges from 40 to 52%.

01:19:25.250 --> 01:19:27.230
It's pretty good.

01:19:27.230 --> 01:19:30.500
And so you can see tensor
parallel starts at 1,

01:19:30.500 --> 01:19:33.740
and then they eventually go up
to 8, and then it caps out at 8.

01:19:33.740 --> 01:19:36.950
And so they're using
tensor parallelism first.

01:19:36.950 --> 01:19:38.810
And then pipeline
parallel stays at 1.

01:19:38.810 --> 01:19:40.640
But once the models
get big enough,

01:19:40.640 --> 01:19:42.050
they can't fit these big models.

01:19:42.050 --> 01:19:47.270
So pipeline parallel has to
increase in order to compensate.

01:19:47.270 --> 01:19:49.970
And then the data
parallel size basically

01:19:49.970 --> 01:19:52.550
starts out as big as
possible and then slowly

01:19:52.550 --> 01:19:56.270
kind of goes down because as we
increase the amount of pipeline

01:19:56.270 --> 01:20:00.260
parallel, this is now consuming,
in some sense, the batch sizes.

01:20:00.260 --> 01:20:02.240
And so you can't
have effectively

01:20:02.240 --> 01:20:04.280
as big of a batch
size if they're

01:20:04.280 --> 01:20:09.230
being used, in some sense,
for pipeline parallel.

01:20:09.230 --> 01:20:14.480
So careful 3D parallelism is
going to give you linear gains

01:20:14.480 --> 01:20:16.950
in aggregate flops.

01:20:16.950 --> 01:20:20.810
So you see, if you do
careful 3D parallelism,

01:20:20.810 --> 01:20:25.200
you can see very flat overall
achieved flops per GPU,

01:20:25.200 --> 01:20:28.760
which is giving you-- if you
add more GPUs linear scaling

01:20:28.760 --> 01:20:31.730
in the total aggregate
throughput, that's great.

01:20:31.730 --> 01:20:34.550
Tensor parallel 8
is often optimal.

01:20:34.550 --> 01:20:36.590
You see this is the
pipeline parallel size

01:20:36.590 --> 01:20:37.920
and the tensor parallel size.

01:20:37.920 --> 01:20:41.340
You see going to 8, 8 with a
batch size of 30-- or sorry,

01:20:41.340 --> 01:20:43.040
batch size of 128 is optimal.

01:20:43.040 --> 01:20:46.560
Even if you have a smaller batch
size, tensor parallel size of 8

01:20:46.560 --> 01:20:49.020
remains optimal.

01:20:49.020 --> 01:20:53.740
And activation recomputation
enables larger batch sizes.

01:20:53.740 --> 01:20:56.400
And remember that larger
batches can in turn

01:20:56.400 --> 01:20:59.470
help you mask overhead
for pipeline parallel.

01:20:59.470 --> 01:21:02.550
So activation recomputation,
even though it's more flops,

01:21:02.550 --> 01:21:03.840
can pay for itself.

01:21:03.840 --> 01:21:09.190
We've seen that story play out
already in flash attention.

01:21:09.190 --> 01:21:09.760
All right.

01:21:09.760 --> 01:21:13.570
So the last part of this
is recent language models,

01:21:13.570 --> 01:21:14.440
what do they do.

01:21:14.440 --> 01:21:16.110
So I've gone
through a few papers

01:21:16.110 --> 01:21:18.540
to look at examples of what
people's parallelization

01:21:18.540 --> 01:21:20.550
strategy is.

01:21:20.550 --> 01:21:24.960
OLMo and the Dolma paper, they
do FSDP for 7 billion parameter

01:21:24.960 --> 01:21:26.010
model.

01:21:26.010 --> 01:21:29.760
DeepSeek, the first
paper does ZeRO stage 1

01:21:29.760 --> 01:21:31.720
with tensor sequence
and pipeline parallel.

01:21:31.720 --> 01:21:35.040
This is the vanilla
thing that I told you.

01:21:35.040 --> 01:21:37.660
V3 actually does something
slightly different.

01:21:37.660 --> 01:21:41.970
They do 16 way pipeline
parallel, 64 way expert

01:21:41.970 --> 01:21:44.250
parallel, which is kind
of tensor parallel,

01:21:44.250 --> 01:21:49.420
and then ZeRO stage 1 for their
data parallelism strategy.

01:21:49.420 --> 01:21:51.470
Yi, which is another
Chinese model,

01:21:51.470 --> 01:21:55.000
does, once again, ZeRO stage 1
tensor and pipeline parallel.

01:21:55.000 --> 01:21:56.890
And Yi-lightning, because
they're doing Mohs,

01:21:56.890 --> 01:22:01.030
replaces tensor parallelism
with expert parallelism.

01:22:01.030 --> 01:22:03.640
The final thing, if
you're interested in state

01:22:03.640 --> 01:22:07.690
of the art distributed
training with lots of details,

01:22:07.690 --> 01:22:10.882
Llama3's report is actually
really interesting to read.

01:22:10.882 --> 01:22:12.340
They have a lot of
detail about how

01:22:12.340 --> 01:22:15.370
they do their networking,
what things happen.

01:22:15.370 --> 01:22:18.590
And you see, once again, the
kinds of things I said before.

01:22:18.590 --> 01:22:21.340
You see a tensor
parallel of 8, you

01:22:21.340 --> 01:22:25.010
see CP or this is
context parallel.

01:22:25.010 --> 01:22:27.162
This is only relevant for
long context training,

01:22:27.162 --> 01:22:28.370
which is this very last step.

01:22:28.370 --> 01:22:29.590
So you can ignore that.

01:22:29.590 --> 01:22:32.650
And you've got pipeline parallel
and data parallel happening

01:22:32.650 --> 01:22:34.705
in these first two phases.

01:22:34.705 --> 01:22:36.580
You can also even ignore
the first stage here

01:22:36.580 --> 01:22:39.100
because that's the small
batch size training that they

01:22:39.100 --> 01:22:41.020
did in order to be stable.

01:22:41.020 --> 01:22:44.000
And if you look at of their
rationale for how they

01:22:44.000 --> 01:22:45.690
do their parallelism strategy.

01:22:45.690 --> 01:22:49.260
You see exactly what I had said
before of basically, all right,

01:22:49.260 --> 01:22:52.910
you want to do TP, CP,
pipeline parallel, and DP

01:22:52.910 --> 01:22:54.980
in that order in terms of
the amount of bandwidth

01:22:54.980 --> 01:22:57.740
that you need, where data
parallel can tolerate

01:22:57.740 --> 01:22:59.960
these long network
latencies, because you

01:22:59.960 --> 01:23:02.950
can do this asynchronous
fetching of sharded model

01:23:02.950 --> 01:23:03.450
weights.

01:23:03.450 --> 01:23:05.390
And so they're
using the strategy

01:23:05.390 --> 01:23:06.920
that I told you
in order to train

01:23:06.920 --> 01:23:09.260
some of the biggest models.

01:23:09.260 --> 01:23:11.810
The funny side
note about Llama3--

01:23:11.810 --> 01:23:13.970
and you may have
heard this in, not

01:23:13.970 --> 01:23:17.330
rumors, but casual
conversation with your friends,

01:23:17.330 --> 01:23:20.990
is there's lots of GPU
failures when you train models

01:23:20.990 --> 01:23:22.550
at a huge scale.

01:23:22.550 --> 01:23:28.400
They had 148 interruptions from
faulty GPUs, totaling about 30%

01:23:28.400 --> 01:23:30.240
of the total interruptions
that they had.

01:23:30.240 --> 01:23:33.090
They had things like unplanned
maintenance of machines.

01:23:33.090 --> 01:23:35.510
And that was 32
different things,

01:23:35.510 --> 01:23:38.070
32 instances of interruptions
for their training.

01:23:38.070 --> 01:23:40.010
And so when you're
training a model this big--

01:23:40.010 --> 01:23:41.880
I've talked about
the algorithms,

01:23:41.880 --> 01:23:44.520
but you also need kind of
fault tolerant architectures

01:23:44.520 --> 01:23:47.220
to be able to deal with
these kinds of things.

01:23:47.220 --> 01:23:50.070
And I've also heard various
stories of people saying

01:23:50.070 --> 01:23:52.200
the even scarier
thing is not actually

01:23:52.200 --> 01:23:55.020
explicit model failures, but
actually data corruption.

01:23:55.020 --> 01:23:57.660
GPUs can silently fail on you
and give you garbage data,

01:23:57.660 --> 01:23:59.640
completely ruining your run.

01:23:59.640 --> 01:24:03.220
And then the last one
example is for Gemma 2.

01:24:03.220 --> 01:24:06.360
And I wanted to end on this
because this is a TPU example.

01:24:06.360 --> 01:24:09.160
They do ZeRO 3, which
is roughly FSDP.

01:24:09.160 --> 01:24:11.070
And then they do
model parallelism

01:24:11.070 --> 01:24:12.070
and data parallelism.

01:24:12.070 --> 01:24:15.480
And so here, as I
said before, the TPUs

01:24:15.480 --> 01:24:18.660
allows them to stretch model
parallelism a little bit

01:24:18.660 --> 01:24:19.860
further.

01:24:19.860 --> 01:24:23.370
So putting it all together,
scaling beyond a certain point

01:24:23.370 --> 01:24:26.500
is going to require multi-GPU,
multi-node parallelism.

01:24:26.500 --> 01:24:28.000
There's no single solution.

01:24:28.000 --> 01:24:31.222
So you want to combine all three
approaches to leverage strength.

01:24:31.222 --> 01:24:33.180
And then there's simple
and interpretable rules

01:24:33.180 --> 01:24:34.597
of thumb for how
you might execute

01:24:34.597 --> 01:24:36.040
this parallelism in practice.

01:24:36.040 --> 01:24:36.640
All right.

01:24:36.640 --> 01:24:38.330
Thank you.
